1
00:00:15,224 --> 00:00:16,933
TOM WEAVER : The one emblematic movie monster

2
00:00:17,017 --> 00:00:19,852
of the 1950s emerged , in 3-D , no less ,

3
00:00:19,937 --> 00:00:21,437
from Universal Pictures ,

4
00:00:21,522 --> 00:00:24,649
the studio which had already led the field for over two decades .

5
00:00:24,733 --> 00:00:26,734
Science fiction expert Bill Warren calls it

6
00:00:26,818 --> 00:00:29,237
" one of the most famous monsters ever created . "

7
00:00:29,321 --> 00:00:33,866
Another sci-fi authority , Bob Burns , says it 's among the best monsters ever designed ,

8
00:00:33,951 --> 00:00:35,910
and that despite its Johnny-come-lately status ,

9
00:00:35,994 --> 00:00:38,871
it fits right in with Frankenstein , The Wolf Man , The Mummy ,

10
00:00:38,956 --> 00:00:41,374
and all the rest of the great movie monsters of old .

11
00:00:41,458 --> 00:00:43,751
The characters in the movie call him The Gill Man .

12
00:00:43,835 --> 00:00:47,129
The people who made the movie affectionately called him The Beastie .

13
00:00:47,214 --> 00:00:49,840
We call him The Creature from the Black Lagoon .

14
00:00:49,925 --> 00:00:53,511
Out of the Devonian Period , when life crawled out of the sea , it came .

15
00:00:53,595 --> 00:00:57,932
From fishlike thing to mammal , from mammal to a man-like horror of the sea

16
00:00:58,016 --> 00:01:03,104
with the heart of a human , filled with hate , and a man-like instinct to love .

17
00:01:03,188 --> 00:01:06,399
My name is Tom Weaver and together we 'll go back to that black lagoon

18
00:01:06,483 --> 00:01:09,026
and take a peek behind the scenes at one of the best and most famous

19
00:01:09,111 --> 00:01:11,904
monster movies from the second-half of the 20th century .

20
00:01:11,989 --> 00:01:15,283
In the beginning , there was William Alland .

21
00:01:15,367 --> 00:01:18,160
Alland was the man who came up with the idea for Creature from the Black Lagoon

22
00:01:18,245 --> 00:01:19,870
and who produced the picture .

23
00:01:19,955 --> 00:01:21,914
Alland was born in Delmar , Delaware ,

24
00:01:21,999 --> 00:01:25,418
and he began his show business career as an actor with a Baltimore troupe .

25
00:01:25,502 --> 00:01:27,587
A short time later , he moved to New York City ,

26
00:01:27,671 --> 00:01:32,258
arriving there with a few belongings , $ 25 , and the ambition to work on Broadway .

27
00:01:32,342 --> 00:01:35,803
He took acting courses , and he did some acting at the Henry Street Settlement House ,

28
00:01:35,887 --> 00:01:38,055
which is where he met Orson Welles .

29
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:39,265
This was in the ' 30s .

30
00:01:39,349 --> 00:01:41,434
And Welles was then on the verge of forming his famous

31
00:01:41,518 --> 00:01:43,811
Mercury Players acting company .

32
00:01:43,895 --> 00:01:46,939
Alland started working with the Mercury Players on stage and on the radio ,

33
00:01:47,024 --> 00:01:50,276
where he was part of the cast of the notorious Halloween , 1938 ,

34
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:52,737
War of the Worlds CBS radio broadcast ,

35
00:01:52,821 --> 00:01:55,573
which was so realistic it caused a panic among listeners ,

36
00:01:55,657 --> 00:01:58,618
and Welles and Alland were nearly thrown into jail .

37
00:01:58,702 --> 00:02:01,287
Alland and Welles continued to associate for years .

38
00:02:01,371 --> 00:02:04,790
Alland also acted in some of Welles ' movies , including Citizen Kane .

39
00:02:04,875 --> 00:02:07,376
Where Alland played the reporter whose face you never see ,

40
00:02:07,461 --> 00:02:09,378
the one who goes through the whole movie in shadow ,

41
00:02:09,463 --> 00:02:13,132
trying to find out what Charles Foster Kane 's dying word , " Rosebud , " meant .

42
00:02:14,134 --> 00:02:16,969
I 'm going to be interrupting myself several times in the next few minutes

43
00:02:17,054 --> 00:02:19,472
while I try to tell you about the genesis of this movie .

44
00:02:19,556 --> 00:02:22,725
So much to say , and only 79 minutes to say it in .

45
00:02:22,809 --> 00:02:25,436
We 're looking here at a shot of Will Rogers Beach in Santa Monica ,

46
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:26,937
a little west of Universal .

47
00:02:27,022 --> 00:02:28,981
William Alland also socialized with Orson Welles ,

48
00:02:29,066 --> 00:02:32,276
and at some point in the ' 40s , perhaps during the making of Citizen Kane ,

49
00:02:32,361 --> 00:02:34,487
he was invited to have dinner at Welles ' home .

50
00:02:34,571 --> 00:02:35,988
It was a little bit of a party , I guess ,

51
00:02:36,073 --> 00:02:38,532
because Orson Welles ' girlfriend was there , Dolores del Rio ,

52
00:02:38,617 --> 00:02:41,035
who was a Mexican actress , and Alland was there ,

53
00:02:41,119 --> 00:02:42,912
and a guy named Gabriel Figueroa ,

54
00:02:42,996 --> 00:02:46,332
who was a Mexican cinematographer , and eventually quite a famous one .

55
00:02:46,416 --> 00:02:49,710
He photographed The Pearl , he shot a lot of Luis Buñuel 's movies ,

56
00:02:49,795 --> 00:02:52,713
he shot John Ford 's The Fugitive with Dolores del Rio ,

57
00:02:52,798 --> 00:02:55,841
and he got an Academy Award nomination for best cinematography

58
00:02:55,926 --> 00:02:59,679
for The Night of the Iguana , 1964 , for director John Huston .

59
00:02:59,763 --> 00:03:03,307
Huston later gave Figueroa one of his last jobs , maybe the last job .

60
00:03:03,392 --> 00:03:06,519
Figueroa photographed Huston 's Under the Volcano in the 1980s ,

61
00:03:06,603 --> 00:03:09,271
when he , Figueroa , was almost 80 .

62
00:03:09,356 --> 00:03:12,775
On the right is Julio López , who later changed his name to Perry Lopez

63
00:03:12,859 --> 00:03:16,654
and became a busy B-movie star and an A-movie character actor .

64
00:03:16,738 --> 00:03:19,615
He played Jack Nicholson 's former partner , Escobar the cop ,

65
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:21,283
in Chinatown and The Two Jakes .

66
00:03:21,368 --> 00:03:24,203
So here they all are at this dinner party at Orson Welles ' home .

67
00:03:24,287 --> 00:03:25,538
Just the four of them , I think .

68
00:03:25,622 --> 00:03:30,084
And Gabriel Figueroa somehow gets onto the subject of a real-life , half-man half-fish

69
00:03:30,168 --> 00:03:32,712
that lives in the Amazon River , near a village .

70
00:03:32,796 --> 00:03:36,465
According to Figueroa , once a year it comes out of the Amazon and claims a maiden ,

71
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:39,969
and then it returns to the Amazon and the village is safe for another year .

72
00:03:40,053 --> 00:03:44,140
Everybody just stared at Figueroa , I guess . He could tell they thought he was kidding .

73
00:03:44,224 --> 00:03:47,601
And he started getting sort of worked up , and he insisted that it was absolutely true

74
00:03:47,686 --> 00:03:50,646
and that the people who live on the Amazon talk about it all the time .

75
00:03:50,731 --> 00:03:52,940
And he said he 'd once seen a photograph of the monster

76
00:03:53,024 --> 00:03:56,235
and that he could get a print of that photograph if they didn 't believe him .

77
00:03:56,319 --> 00:03:59,780
Notice the little rubber pinkie claw , instead of scratching the sand ,

78
00:03:59,865 --> 00:04:01,449
just bends out of shape .

79
00:04:01,533 --> 00:04:04,326
For about five minutes Figueroa kept talking very earnestly about

80
00:04:04,411 --> 00:04:08,914
this half-man half-fish that he said was not a myth , that actually existed .

81
00:04:08,999 --> 00:04:10,958
I have no idea whether he was on the level or not ,

82
00:04:11,042 --> 00:04:13,419
and I don 't know if anybody there ended up believing him ,

83
00:04:13,503 --> 00:04:18,215
but I do know that that little anecdote got lodged in the back of William Alland 's brain .

84
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:20,301
About 10 years later , Alland landed a job

85
00:04:20,385 --> 00:04:22,595
as a movie producer at Universal International .

86
00:04:22,679 --> 00:04:24,138
He made a lot of Westerns early on ,

87
00:04:24,222 --> 00:04:26,640
he made a Tony Curtis Arabian Nights sort of thing

88
00:04:26,725 --> 00:04:28,184
called The Prince Who Was a Thief .

89
00:04:28,268 --> 00:04:31,270
All moneymakers , I 'm sure , but nothing world-shaking .

90
00:04:31,354 --> 00:04:33,647
Interrupting myself again , here 's a good example

91
00:04:33,732 --> 00:04:36,358
of how movies go together like big jigsaw puzzles .

92
00:04:36,443 --> 00:04:38,861
This is a shot of a speedboat racing across the waters of

93
00:04:38,945 --> 00:04:42,740
Portuguese Bend in California , shot September 30 , 1953 .

94
00:04:44,785 --> 00:04:49,121
Now we 're on the process stage at Universal , a month later , October 29 .

95
00:04:49,206 --> 00:04:51,665
But the image you see in the background on the process screen

96
00:04:51,750 --> 00:04:54,543
was shot at Portuguese Bend on October 5 .

97
00:04:57,923 --> 00:04:59,840
And now we 're 2,500 miles east ,

98
00:04:59,925 --> 00:05:04,094
beneath the surface of Wakulla Springs in Florida , on October 14 .

99
00:05:04,179 --> 00:05:07,097
So this little nothing scene was shot in three different places ,

100
00:05:07,182 --> 00:05:10,518
on four or more different days , two different directors ,

101
00:05:10,602 --> 00:05:12,394
and the three characters in the scene were played ,

102
00:05:12,479 --> 00:05:14,063
or represented , I guess I should say ,

103
00:05:14,147 --> 00:05:18,150
by seven different people . It 's amazing all the work that goes into making a movie .

104
00:05:19,236 --> 00:05:22,154
Anyway , Alland 's there at Universal , making picture after picture ,

105
00:05:22,239 --> 00:05:24,865
and then he starts coming up with ideas for science-fiction movies .

106
00:05:24,950 --> 00:05:27,076
He dreamed up the idea for It Came from Outer Space

107
00:05:27,327 --> 00:05:29,411
and assigned Ray Bradbury to write a treatment .

108
00:05:29,496 --> 00:05:32,581
And right about that same time , maybe as little as just a few weeks later ,

109
00:05:32,666 --> 00:05:35,376
he himself wrote up and submitted to his studio bosses

110
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:37,670
a story idea called The Sea Monster .

111
00:05:37,754 --> 00:05:41,131
It was just a little three-page thing , where he spent the first page telling the story

112
00:05:41,216 --> 00:05:43,843
of that real-life dinner conversation with Figueroa .

113
00:05:43,927 --> 00:05:47,763
And then he pitched the idea that he should make a movie about that kind of monster .

114
00:05:47,931 --> 00:05:50,975
He wanted the movie to start off with a scene of a dinner conversation ,

115
00:05:51,059 --> 00:05:53,310
just like the one they 'd had in real life with Figueroa ,

116
00:05:53,562 --> 00:05:55,896
and then the movie would go on to show a small expedition

117
00:05:55,981 --> 00:05:58,232
entering an unexplored region of the Amazon ,

118
00:05:58,316 --> 00:06:00,734
accompanied , of course , by a beautiful blonde .

119
00:06:00,861 --> 00:06:03,696
The man-fish spots the blonde and gets a crush on her , and ,

120
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:06,824
here 's where Alland 's imagination started running away with him a little ,

121
00:06:06,950 --> 00:06:09,201
there was also supposed to be a bad guy on the expedition ,

122
00:06:09,286 --> 00:06:12,454
who wants to catch the creature , and he uses the girl as bait .

123
00:06:12,622 --> 00:06:15,457
Alland 's memo on The Sea Monster ended with the suggestion

124
00:06:15,542 --> 00:06:17,042
that they use one of two endings .

125
00:06:17,127 --> 00:06:19,336
In one ending , the girl is abducted by the creature ,

126
00:06:19,462 --> 00:06:22,047
and her boyfriend has to rescue her and the monster gets killed .

127
00:06:22,173 --> 00:06:25,426
And in the other ending , the creature is captured and brought back to civilization ,

128
00:06:25,635 --> 00:06:29,555
but it escapes and starts terrorizing a little seaport on the South American coast .

129
00:06:29,681 --> 00:06:32,725
Alland wrote , " Needless to say , the monster 's end is brought about

130
00:06:32,809 --> 00:06:36,437
" by his desire for the blonde-haired girl of the expedition . "

131
00:06:36,521 --> 00:06:38,939
Well , you know what that is . That 's King Kong .

132
00:06:39,024 --> 00:06:42,568
Alland took the idea of a man-fish from that conversation with Figueroa

133
00:06:42,652 --> 00:06:46,572
and he got most of the rest of his original story idea from King Kong .

134
00:06:46,656 --> 00:06:49,742
When they made Creature , they did go with the first ending , the simple ending

135
00:06:49,826 --> 00:06:52,161
where the hero rescues the girl and the creature is killed .

136
00:06:52,329 --> 00:06:55,831
But then a few months later , Alland produced a sequel , Revenge of the Creature ,

137
00:06:55,916 --> 00:06:58,918
which used the second ending . The creature is brought back to civilization ,

138
00:06:59,002 --> 00:07:01,420
and it escapes , and it abducts the girl it loves .

139
00:07:01,546 --> 00:07:03,297
In Revenge of the Creature it 's a different girl ,

140
00:07:03,381 --> 00:07:05,090
a different actress from the original Creature .

141
00:07:05,175 --> 00:07:08,344
In Creature , the girl is Julie Adams , but in Revenge , it 's Lori Nelson ,

142
00:07:08,428 --> 00:07:11,597
who , true to Alland 's original story , was a blonde .

143
00:07:11,723 --> 00:07:15,768
I interviewed Alland back around 1995 , two years before he passed away .

144
00:07:15,852 --> 00:07:16,977
At the time when I talked to him ,

145
00:07:17,062 --> 00:07:19,980
I 'd recently watched Creature and Revenge of the Creature back-to-back

146
00:07:20,065 --> 00:07:22,608
and realized , for the first time , that the story was King Kong

147
00:07:22,734 --> 00:07:24,610
set on the water instead of the land .

148
00:07:24,694 --> 00:07:27,196
I asked Alland if he 'd gotten his ideas from King Kong .

149
00:07:27,322 --> 00:07:30,115
And he immediately started laughing . He said he did , absolutely ,

150
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:34,828
that that was the whole idea , his idea , to redo King Kong , but with a watermonster .

151
00:07:34,913 --> 00:07:38,290
The first writer William Alland assigned to the movie was a fellow named Maurice Zimm ,

152
00:07:38,375 --> 00:07:42,503
who turned in a 59-page draft , titled Black Lagoon , in early December , 1952 .

153
00:07:42,587 --> 00:07:45,839
And it 's sufficiently different from the movie to be worth mentioning here .

154
00:07:45,924 --> 00:07:48,926
In this draft , Dr. Lyman Reed , an undersea explorer ,

155
00:07:49,010 --> 00:07:52,137
risks his life in various underwater adventures in order to raise money

156
00:07:52,222 --> 00:07:55,307
to form an expedition and search the Amazon for the Pisces Man ,

157
00:07:55,392 --> 00:07:57,810
a man-fish that is reported to live there .

158
00:07:59,729 --> 00:08:02,147
We 're looking now , by the way , at footage shot on location

159
00:08:02,232 --> 00:08:04,024
at the Hermosa Beach Aquarium .

160
00:08:04,109 --> 00:08:06,860
Julie Adams and a crew went there during preproduction .

161
00:08:07,737 --> 00:08:10,990
Dr. Reed 's girlfriend , Kay , an heiress , puts up the money herself ,

162
00:08:11,074 --> 00:08:13,283
but insists on going along with the expedition .

163
00:08:13,451 --> 00:08:17,121
Unbeknownst to Kay , her guardian hires an adventurer named Ted Clayton

164
00:08:17,205 --> 00:08:20,290
to find a way to also join the expedition and secretly watch over her .

165
00:08:21,001 --> 00:08:24,586
On the expedition up the Amazon are Dr. Reed , Ted , Kay ,

166
00:08:24,671 --> 00:08:26,672
Kay 's doting companion , Winnie ,

167
00:08:26,756 --> 00:08:30,592
and an explorer named Carl Sloan , who 's got " bad guy " written all over him .

168
00:08:30,677 --> 00:08:34,680
The explorers set traps for the Pisces Man , but it avoids them and weeks go by .

169
00:08:34,764 --> 00:08:38,392
Sloan finally gets fed up , chloroforms Kay and places her on a raft ,

170
00:08:38,518 --> 00:08:41,729
using her as bait for the Pisces Man . The Pisces Man appears .

171
00:08:41,813 --> 00:08:45,065
It has a " huge , fascinating , unbelievable head ,

172
00:08:45,191 --> 00:08:48,068
" with a throat sac like a frog 's and scalloped gills

173
00:08:48,153 --> 00:08:52,531
" that , at a distance , resemble the bobbed hair of a knight of old . "

174
00:08:52,615 --> 00:08:55,284
The Pisces Man kills Sloan and gets away with Kay ,

175
00:08:55,368 --> 00:08:58,704
but Ted follows them to the monster 's grotto and subdues the Pisces Man

176
00:08:58,788 --> 00:09:02,041
by slapping a wrestling hold on it until it passes out .

177
00:09:02,125 --> 00:09:05,377
Kay feels sorry for the Pisces Man , which is locked in a water-filled tank

178
00:09:05,462 --> 00:09:09,339
and brought back to civilization . The tank is taken off the boat and put on a plane .

179
00:09:09,549 --> 00:09:11,925
But Kay sneaks onto the plane when no one else is aboard ,

180
00:09:12,010 --> 00:09:14,803
and unlocks the tank so that the Pisces Man can escape .

181
00:09:14,888 --> 00:09:18,474
The Pisces Man " comes slowly toward her , looms over her ,

182
00:09:18,558 --> 00:09:20,976
" and touches the whiteness of her throat , ever so gently .

183
00:09:21,102 --> 00:09:24,021
" Then , with his eyes still on her , he leaves the plane . "

184
00:09:24,314 --> 00:09:28,317
The military throws out a dragnet , but the Pisces Man is able to elude the searchers .

185
00:09:28,568 --> 00:09:31,278
At one point , it climbs through a window into a house to hide ,

186
00:09:31,362 --> 00:09:35,449
sees a small child sleeping , and adjusts the kid 's blanket before it leaves .

187
00:09:35,658 --> 00:09:39,203
But when the Pisces Man tries to get back into the water by crossing a stretch of beach ,

188
00:09:39,287 --> 00:09:41,371
Dr. Reed shoots him over and over and over .

189
00:09:41,498 --> 00:09:44,374
Reed yells out , " If I can 't have him alive , then I 'll have him dead . "

190
00:09:44,501 --> 00:09:47,961
The Pisces Man crawls into the water , which instantly starts churning

191
00:09:48,046 --> 00:09:49,671
because piranha are attacking it .

192
00:09:49,756 --> 00:09:53,342
Kay dumps Dr. Reed and flies back home with Ted , instead .

193
00:09:54,177 --> 00:09:58,514
The initial Zimm treatment is too much like King Kong , apart from all its other flaws .

194
00:09:58,681 --> 00:10:02,101
But when this early draft was discussed in a December , 1952 , memo ,

195
00:10:02,185 --> 00:10:05,813
the memo writer , who didn 't sign his name , wrote that he was sure it could be developed

196
00:10:05,897 --> 00:10:08,565
into " a very fine horror movie . "

197
00:10:08,691 --> 00:10:10,359
The memo writer , whoever he was ,

198
00:10:10,527 --> 00:10:13,028
also mentions discussing the script with William Alland ,

199
00:10:13,196 --> 00:10:15,030
who said that he would change the ending

200
00:10:15,115 --> 00:10:17,950
" to provide a means of possibly continuing this into sequels

201
00:10:18,034 --> 00:10:19,535
" by keeping the monster alive ,

202
00:10:19,702 --> 00:10:22,704
" or at least leaving his fate in doubt at the end of the picture . "

203
00:10:23,206 --> 00:10:26,291
So as early as 1952 , plans were already being tentatively made

204
00:10:26,626 --> 00:10:28,502
to turn Black Lagoon into a series .

205
00:10:35,218 --> 00:10:38,303
There were a lot of monster 's-eye-view shots in It Came from Outer Space ,

206
00:10:38,388 --> 00:10:41,723
produced by William Alland and directed by Jack Arnold several months earlier .

207
00:10:41,808 --> 00:10:44,226
So Alland and Arnold , again teamed on this picture ,

208
00:10:44,394 --> 00:10:47,271
used that gimmick here , and then again later , in Tarantula .

209
00:10:47,397 --> 00:10:50,607
They also teased the audience by keeping The Gill Man mostly off screen

210
00:10:50,692 --> 00:10:51,817
in its first couple of scenes .

211
00:10:51,901 --> 00:10:55,070
We don 't get to really see the creature until a third of the way through the movie .

212
00:10:55,196 --> 00:10:58,157
For the roar of The Gill Man , which you 'll hear for the first time in a moment ,

213
00:10:58,241 --> 00:11:02,411
the Universal sound technicians reportedly experimented with a lot of strange noises ,

214
00:11:02,537 --> 00:11:06,498
from a foghorn blown underwater , to an opera recording played at low speed .

215
00:11:06,958 --> 00:11:09,751
To be honest , I don 't know what they eventually did use for the roar ,

216
00:11:09,919 --> 00:11:12,838
but I do know that for the Steven Spielberg TV movie Duel ,

217
00:11:12,922 --> 00:11:16,091
a distorted Gill Man roar is used as the sound of the truck crashing .

218
00:11:16,217 --> 00:11:18,468
That 's just great , that big baseball-mitt sized hand

219
00:11:18,595 --> 00:11:20,929
reaching up and grabbing Rodd Redwing by the head .

220
00:11:21,097 --> 00:11:24,391
Everybody I know who saw this movie on its original run in 1954 ,

221
00:11:24,475 --> 00:11:26,727
tells me this was one of the scariest scenes in the movie .

222
00:11:32,984 --> 00:11:36,904
Here 's our first shot of the Rita . Most of the rest of the movie takes place on the Rita .

223
00:11:36,988 --> 00:11:40,490
Back to the script of Black Lagoon . Maurice Zimm handed in his draft ,

224
00:11:40,617 --> 00:11:43,952
and then a writer named Leo Lieberman came in and worked for about two months .

225
00:11:44,162 --> 00:11:47,873
But the story still wasn 't in very good shape . In fact , according to Arthur Ross ,

226
00:11:48,166 --> 00:11:52,169
the Universal front office had turned it down and said they weren 't going to do the picture .

227
00:11:52,295 --> 00:11:56,298
That was the point at which Alland turned to Ross , a Chicago-born screenwriter ,

228
00:11:56,424 --> 00:11:59,051
who did a lot of work whipping the story into shape .

229
00:11:59,469 --> 00:12:02,471
Ross took a look at what had been written and he quickly realized

230
00:12:02,555 --> 00:12:05,557
" that it was an imitation of films that had been made in the genre

231
00:12:05,642 --> 00:12:07,476
" at Universal for 25 years .

232
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:09,728
" The only difference was that it was an undersea creature

233
00:12:09,812 --> 00:12:12,648
" instead of a mummy , or Frankenstein , or Dracula . "

234
00:12:12,815 --> 00:12:16,235
Ross was , at the time , reading the book The Silent World by Jacques Cousteau ,

235
00:12:16,319 --> 00:12:18,070
and reading that book gave him the idea

236
00:12:18,154 --> 00:12:20,864
that the scientist in the movie should be the hero , not the villain .

237
00:12:21,366 --> 00:12:25,452
Remember , neither of the two scientists in Maurice Zimm 's Black Lagoon was a hero .

238
00:12:25,536 --> 00:12:28,455
One was a publicity-seeking nut , and the other was the movie 's villain ,

239
00:12:28,539 --> 00:12:30,666
a real Snidely Whiplash type .

240
00:12:30,833 --> 00:12:33,252
What Ross wanted to do was make the scientist

241
00:12:33,336 --> 00:12:37,589
" a humanist who enquires of nature rather than dictating to it or exploiting it .

242
00:12:37,674 --> 00:12:40,717
" He 's the one who holds out for not harming the creature . "

243
00:12:40,802 --> 00:12:43,762
Alland understood right away what Ross was trying to do .

244
00:12:43,846 --> 00:12:47,140
According to Ross , Alland knew that the first approach had failed miserably ,

245
00:12:47,225 --> 00:12:49,351
the old-fashioned script that they were trying to work with .

246
00:12:49,435 --> 00:12:50,894
Ross told me ,

247
00:12:50,979 --> 00:12:53,855
" No matter what I 'm doing , whether I 'm writing something just to make a living ,

248
00:12:53,982 --> 00:12:57,192
" or something I really believe is important , I never write down to an audience .

249
00:12:57,277 --> 00:12:59,778
" I don 't believe in that . Audiences are not stupid .

250
00:12:59,862 --> 00:13:02,698
" They may not be geniuses , they may not be specialists in fields ,

251
00:13:02,824 --> 00:13:06,410
" but they are reasonably intelligent people , in the main , and for that reason

252
00:13:06,494 --> 00:13:10,122
" I always felt that I would present what I thought was not only a melodramatic device ,

253
00:13:10,206 --> 00:13:12,416
" but also a study of characters in conflict .

254
00:13:12,542 --> 00:13:15,711
" The conflict , in this case , would be these people versus the creature .

255
00:13:15,795 --> 00:13:18,463
" The more they attack him , the more he attacks them .

256
00:13:18,548 --> 00:13:20,924
" I wrote intelligent people doing intelligent things .

257
00:13:21,050 --> 00:13:24,094
" Everyone , that is , except for the aggressive person , Richard Denning ,

258
00:13:24,220 --> 00:13:25,971
" who wanted the creature dead and mounted ,

259
00:13:26,055 --> 00:13:28,390
" considering that just as good as live and left alone .

260
00:13:28,474 --> 00:13:32,227
" He was unintelligent and he was arrogant , and he caused the difficulties .

261
00:13:32,562 --> 00:13:35,230
" And those difficulties finally had to be rectified in violence ,

262
00:13:35,398 --> 00:13:38,275
" because by that time , the creature was a violent and beset thing

263
00:13:38,359 --> 00:13:40,777
" that had no choice but to fight them . "

264
00:13:42,030 --> 00:13:45,324
After Ross finished up , one last writer , Harry Essex , was brought in .

265
00:13:45,408 --> 00:13:48,243
A few months earlier , Essex had had the very simple job

266
00:13:48,369 --> 00:13:51,246
of turning a 111-page Ray Bradbury treatment

267
00:13:51,414 --> 00:13:54,333
into the screenplay of It Came from Outer Space , which he did ,

268
00:13:54,417 --> 00:13:57,336
and undeservedly got a solo screenplay credit for it .

269
00:13:57,420 --> 00:14:00,505
In subsequent years , Essex was more than happy to try to take all the credit

270
00:14:00,590 --> 00:14:02,007
for It Came from Outer Space ,

271
00:14:02,091 --> 00:14:05,135
and I 'm afraid he liked to try to take all the credit for Black Lagoon , too .

272
00:14:05,303 --> 00:14:07,304
Writers had been working on Black Lagoon for months ,

273
00:14:07,430 --> 00:14:09,264
many drafts of the screenplay had been written ,

274
00:14:09,432 --> 00:14:11,767
and Arthur Ross had gotten it all whipped into shape .

275
00:14:11,851 --> 00:14:16,438
But to hear Essex tell it , when he was hired , all Universal gave him to work with was

276
00:14:16,564 --> 00:14:19,399
" a very , very poorly-written short story .

277
00:14:19,525 --> 00:14:22,986
" Just the basic idea of a fish that had been discovered in the jungle .

278
00:14:23,071 --> 00:14:26,323
" Universal had bought the story for very little money , and assigned me to it .

279
00:14:26,407 --> 00:14:27,741
" And I was bitter and angry .

280
00:14:27,825 --> 00:14:30,702
" I didn 't want to do anything with a title like Creature from the Black Lagoon .

281
00:14:30,787 --> 00:14:32,871
" It was an embarrassment to me . "

282
00:14:32,955 --> 00:14:36,833
I should add , at this point , that the title was just Black Lagoon when Essex worked on it ,

283
00:14:36,918 --> 00:14:40,545
but I 'm sure you 're already taking all these comments of his with a grain of salt .

284
00:14:40,630 --> 00:14:44,299
Essex went on to tell me , " Universal pleaded with me to do the picture .

285
00:14:44,384 --> 00:14:46,927
" And so I began to redevelop the whole damn thing .

286
00:14:47,053 --> 00:14:50,639
" It 's pretty much formula for the kind of horror stories we used to do in those days .

287
00:14:50,807 --> 00:14:54,142
" Except in this particular case , I added the Beauty and the Beast theme .

288
00:14:54,310 --> 00:14:56,770
" The whole idea was to give the creature a kind of humanity .

289
00:14:56,896 --> 00:14:59,564
" All he wants is to love the girl , but everybody 's chasing him .

290
00:14:59,690 --> 00:15:03,193
" It 's an old formula of mine that I 've used with great success . "

291
00:15:03,903 --> 00:15:05,779
( SIGHING ) Harry , Harry , Harry ...

292
00:15:06,322 --> 00:15:09,658
Some interesting touches in the various scripts that never made it to the screen .

293
00:15:09,742 --> 00:15:12,619
For one thing , the original intent was to keep The Gill Man off camera

294
00:15:12,703 --> 00:15:14,371
until at least the middle of the movie .

295
00:15:14,497 --> 00:15:17,040
Universal ended up spending so much money on The Gill Man costume

296
00:15:17,125 --> 00:15:18,708
that , I guess , understandably ,

297
00:15:18,835 --> 00:15:21,420
they finally decided they wanted to show him early and often .

298
00:15:21,587 --> 00:15:25,132
But all you see of him in the first halves of the early scripts are flash shots ,

299
00:15:25,216 --> 00:15:27,759
you see him in silhouette , you see him in shadow , et cetera .

300
00:15:27,885 --> 00:15:30,429
And there was an evocative scene , also not filmed ,

301
00:15:30,513 --> 00:15:33,723
where the captain of the Rita talks about the fable of a beautiful mermaid

302
00:15:33,808 --> 00:15:36,601
who once lived in one of the Amazon 's hidden lagoons .

303
00:15:36,686 --> 00:15:39,563
" It was a very beautiful legend about how this mermaid ,

304
00:15:39,689 --> 00:15:42,941
" she failed in love with a man on a ship . And nobody will believe him .

305
00:15:43,109 --> 00:15:45,610
" And then one day , when the time came for him to go away ,

306
00:15:45,695 --> 00:15:49,906
" she followed him , far out into the Amazon , only to lose her way

307
00:15:49,991 --> 00:15:51,950
" and be devoured by the crocodiles .

308
00:15:52,034 --> 00:15:57,205
" Who knows ? Maybe this mermaid , she was even the wife of this half-man half-fish . "

309
00:15:57,540 --> 00:15:59,875
That dialogue was designed to be heard as narration

310
00:16:00,001 --> 00:16:02,169
over a montage of water and jungle images .

311
00:16:02,253 --> 00:16:05,714
And it would have explained The Gill Man 's fascination with Julie Adams .

312
00:16:05,798 --> 00:16:07,466
Now , I 'm trying to tell the story behind Creature

313
00:16:07,550 --> 00:16:09,050
in some sort of chronological order ,

314
00:16:09,177 --> 00:16:12,137
but here I wanna jump ahead and mention that according to Chris Mueller ,

315
00:16:12,221 --> 00:16:15,474
the makeup department sculptor who sculpted the head and hands of The Gill Man ,

316
00:16:15,558 --> 00:16:18,351
when Universal was getting ready to make the second Creature movie ,

317
00:16:18,478 --> 00:16:22,022
there was some talk of having The Gill Man and a female creature in the sequel .

318
00:16:22,106 --> 00:16:25,609
Needless to say , there is no female creature in the sequel , Revenge of the Creature ,

319
00:16:25,693 --> 00:16:28,487
nor have I seen a draft of the Revenge of the Creature script

320
00:16:28,571 --> 00:16:31,531
that mentions a female creature , and I think I 've seen all of them .

321
00:16:31,616 --> 00:16:34,493
So , I guess it was just an idea being kicked around .

322
00:16:34,619 --> 00:16:36,786
I 'll talk a little more about that later .

323
00:16:37,497 --> 00:16:39,206
( GROANS ) Eight days .

324
00:16:39,874 --> 00:16:42,042
All we dig up are enough stones to pave a road .

325
00:16:42,126 --> 00:16:44,377
WEAVER : All the scenes in and around Dr. Maia 's camp ,

326
00:16:44,462 --> 00:16:46,963
the fossilized arm sticking out of the rock wall ,

327
00:16:47,173 --> 00:16:49,341
the scene of The Gill Man killing the natives in the tent ,

328
00:16:49,550 --> 00:16:52,677
and now these digging scenes , were all shot on the Universal back lot ,

329
00:16:52,762 --> 00:16:54,596
at a spot called Sierra Canyon .

330
00:16:54,722 --> 00:16:58,183
A few months earlier , Jack Arnold was shooting another movie in Sierra Canyon ,

331
00:16:58,267 --> 00:16:59,768
It Came from Outer Space .

332
00:16:59,936 --> 00:17:02,354
Most of the desert exteriors for It Came from Outer Space

333
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:04,397
were shot on location in the Mojave Desert .

334
00:17:04,524 --> 00:17:08,068
But when Richard Carlson goes down into the meteor crater and finds the spaceship ,

335
00:17:08,152 --> 00:17:10,403
those scenes were shot right here in Sierra Canyon .

336
00:17:10,488 --> 00:17:13,573
They avoided showing the water and all the trees , of course , because in that movie

337
00:17:13,658 --> 00:17:16,868
Carlson was supposedly in a crater in the middle of the Arizona desert .

338
00:17:16,953 --> 00:17:18,912
Over hundreds of years , perhaps over thousands of years ,

339
00:17:18,996 --> 00:17:20,539
the current would break it up .

340
00:17:20,623 --> 00:17:21,831
If there is a dead end ...

341
00:17:21,916 --> 00:17:23,542
WEAVER : Ernest Nims , who was the supervisor

342
00:17:23,626 --> 00:17:25,627
of the editorial department at Universal ,

343
00:17:25,711 --> 00:17:27,170
often made script suggestions .

344
00:17:27,255 --> 00:17:28,922
And one recommendation he made for Creature

345
00:17:29,006 --> 00:17:30,757
was in connection with the early scene

346
00:17:30,841 --> 00:17:34,094
where The Gill Man 's arm reached up out of the Amazon for Julie Adams ' ankle

347
00:17:34,178 --> 00:17:35,971
as she stood on the riverbank .

348
00:17:36,055 --> 00:17:39,849
In a memo , he asked , " Is the creature that we see in the inland water

349
00:17:39,934 --> 00:17:43,562
" the same as the one in the Black Lagoon ? This will be confusing to the audience .

350
00:17:43,646 --> 00:17:45,814
" Why not show the creature following the boat

351
00:17:45,940 --> 00:17:48,316
" when they take the trip up the river to the Black Lagoon ?

352
00:17:48,609 --> 00:17:53,488
" This would not only give added suspense , but would clarify the story point . "

353
00:17:53,614 --> 00:17:55,156
And , you know , that was a good suggestion .

354
00:17:55,241 --> 00:17:58,326
The movie 's famous now , and we know there 's only one Gill Man ,

355
00:17:58,494 --> 00:18:01,329
but in 1954 , when people were seeing it for the first time ,

356
00:18:01,455 --> 00:18:04,374
a Gill Man in Dr. Maia 's camp and another in the Black Lagoon

357
00:18:04,500 --> 00:18:05,875
might have been misleading .

358
00:18:05,960 --> 00:18:08,712
So , sure enough , somebody added a shot to the script .

359
00:18:08,838 --> 00:18:12,591
As the Rita chugs toward the Black Lagoon , there 's a shot of The Gill Man following .

360
00:18:12,675 --> 00:18:15,135
It was a high shot down from the deck of the boat ,

361
00:18:15,219 --> 00:18:17,137
showing The Gill Man just below the surface .

362
00:18:17,221 --> 00:18:21,474
" A long , shark-like shadow , slithering through the water , follows the vessel .

363
00:18:21,559 --> 00:18:25,770
" As he lifts one hand out of the water , we recognize it as the green , taloned thing

364
00:18:25,855 --> 00:18:29,482
" we had seen poking out of the inland water at the geological camp . "

365
00:18:29,734 --> 00:18:34,571
Okay , fine , so the shot 's in the movie , but then , watch this cut right here in a second .

366
00:18:35,531 --> 00:18:38,199
That 's where the shot was before somebody took it out .

367
00:18:38,326 --> 00:18:40,535
You can even hear the music abruptly jump ahead .

368
00:18:40,703 --> 00:18:43,580
They must have taken it out at the last minute , and they didn 't have the time ,

369
00:18:43,664 --> 00:18:45,915
or maybe they just didn 't have the inclination , to fix the music .

370
00:18:46,042 --> 00:18:47,917
They just let it skip ahead very noticeably .

371
00:18:48,002 --> 00:18:51,171
Who took the shot out , and why , I have no idea .

372
00:18:53,299 --> 00:18:56,301
Universal briefly considered shooting Black Lagoon in Eastmancolor .

373
00:18:56,385 --> 00:18:59,346
And of all the Universal sci-fi movies of the ' 50s ,

374
00:18:59,430 --> 00:19:02,557
this is the one that really would have benefited from color , I think .

375
00:19:02,725 --> 00:19:04,309
Creature would have been dynamite in color .

376
00:19:04,393 --> 00:19:09,022
In fact , if the colorization fad hadn 't died out , and if they finally could have gotten it right ,

377
00:19:09,106 --> 00:19:12,233
I would have had no objection , whatsoever , to seeing Creature colorized .

378
00:19:12,526 --> 00:19:15,987
When I told Bill Alland that Creature and Tarantula and some of his other pictures

379
00:19:16,072 --> 00:19:18,657
would have been great in color , he said exactly the same thing ,

380
00:19:18,741 --> 00:19:22,327
that he would love to see Creature colorized , he said it would look fabulous in color .

381
00:19:22,411 --> 00:19:25,830
But I guess the expense of shooting in color worried Universal .

382
00:19:25,915 --> 00:19:30,669
In color and 3D , they projected that Creature would wind up costing them $ 750,000 .

383
00:19:30,753 --> 00:19:33,922
So , pardon the pun , they scaled Creature down ,

384
00:19:34,006 --> 00:19:35,674
made it in black-and-white 3D ,

385
00:19:35,758 --> 00:19:38,176
which they figured would cost them $ 650,000 .

386
00:19:38,260 --> 00:19:39,928
What are you going to shoot at ?

387
00:19:41,305 --> 00:19:42,972
Does it make any difference ?

388
00:19:43,057 --> 00:19:44,391
WEAVER : This is a little embarrassing , maybe ,

389
00:19:44,475 --> 00:19:45,684
to the memory of Jack Arnold ,

390
00:19:45,768 --> 00:19:48,603
and I 'm jumping ahead of myself again , but I 'll point it out anyway .

391
00:19:48,688 --> 00:19:52,315
On the first day of shooting , Tuesday , October 6 , 1953 ,

392
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:54,943
the very first shot was the upcoming shot of the Rita

393
00:19:55,027 --> 00:19:58,405
squeezing its way through the narrow opening that leads into the Black Lagoon ,

394
00:19:58,489 --> 00:20:01,199
the paradise from which no man has ever returned .

395
00:20:01,283 --> 00:20:04,786
So Jack Arnold lines up his first shot on the first day of the movie , and ...

396
00:20:05,371 --> 00:20:06,371
Why , it 's a blooper .

397
00:20:06,455 --> 00:20:09,165
What 's that telephone pole doing on the left-hand side of the screen ?

398
00:20:14,171 --> 00:20:15,964
It 's impossible .

399
00:20:16,132 --> 00:20:19,134
WEAVER : Much as I 'd like to , there may not be time to talk about all of the actors

400
00:20:19,218 --> 00:20:20,385
in Creature from the Black Lagoon .

401
00:20:20,469 --> 00:20:23,471
I 've got too much information here about other aspects of the production .

402
00:20:23,556 --> 00:20:25,765
But one I have to talk about is Julie Adams .

403
00:20:26,058 --> 00:20:28,601
Other actresses made more sci-fi movies in the ' 50s ,

404
00:20:28,686 --> 00:20:32,063
Mara Corday , Beverly Garland , Faith Domergue , dozens of others .

405
00:20:32,148 --> 00:20:35,316
And Julie Adams just made the one . But by appearing with the creature ,

406
00:20:35,484 --> 00:20:38,153
the most recognizable of ' 50s movie monsters ,

407
00:20:38,237 --> 00:20:40,822
Julie Adams instantly jumped right to the front of the line .

408
00:20:40,906 --> 00:20:43,074
She was born Betty May Adams in Iowa ,

409
00:20:43,159 --> 00:20:46,453
and she knew she wanted to be an actress from the days of grade-school plays .

410
00:20:46,662 --> 00:20:51,332
In California , to pursue her career goal , she took speech lessons to lose her Iowa accent ,

411
00:20:51,459 --> 00:20:55,003
and played most of her early roles in cheap Westerns for a little company called Lippert ,

412
00:20:55,129 --> 00:20:56,921
each of them made in less than a week .

413
00:20:57,006 --> 00:20:59,632
At that point , she was still using her real name , Betty Adams ,

414
00:20:59,717 --> 00:21:02,927
but at Universal she became Julia Adams , and then Julie Adams ,

415
00:21:03,012 --> 00:21:06,347
and she co-starred in almost two dozen movies there in the ' 50s .

416
00:21:06,432 --> 00:21:09,976
A famous sculptor proclaimed that she possessed the most perfect legs in the world ,

417
00:21:10,102 --> 00:21:13,646
so Universal claimed that they insured her legs for $ 500,000 .

418
00:21:13,814 --> 00:21:16,733
That 's an old , old publicity trick that went back to the ' 40s .

419
00:21:16,859 --> 00:21:19,944
Betty Grable 's legs were insured for a million . And it 's even used today .

420
00:21:20,029 --> 00:21:23,823
Just the other day , Jennifer Lopez insured her legs for 400 million .

421
00:21:23,949 --> 00:21:27,535
Anyway , she had these great legs that were never seen in any of the movies she made ,

422
00:21:27,620 --> 00:21:29,037
mostly Westerns and things like that ,

423
00:21:29,121 --> 00:21:31,623
so , Universal gave her the bathing-suit lead in Creature .

424
00:21:31,707 --> 00:21:35,293
And that instantly became her claim to fame , the movie she 's now best remembered for ,

425
00:21:35,544 --> 00:21:38,671
which is a " distinction " that she takes with humor .

426
00:21:38,923 --> 00:21:41,216
More humor sometimes than at other times , perhaps ,

427
00:21:41,342 --> 00:21:43,301
but she 's always been a good sport about it .

428
00:21:43,385 --> 00:21:45,929
She worked a lot in television , including The Jimmy Stewart Show ,

429
00:21:46,013 --> 00:21:48,264
as Jimmy 's Stewart 's wife , and Yancy Derringer ,

430
00:21:48,390 --> 00:21:49,933
and a recurring part on Murder , She Wrote ,

431
00:21:50,017 --> 00:21:52,352
and she 's still working in front of the cameras today ,

432
00:21:52,478 --> 00:21:54,562
most recently in the documentary about Creature

433
00:21:54,688 --> 00:21:56,314
which you 'll find at the end of this movie .

434
00:21:56,440 --> 00:21:59,484
Adams has also made a few other science fiction and horror movies ,

435
00:21:59,568 --> 00:22:01,903
including The Underwater City , with William Lundigan ,

436
00:22:02,029 --> 00:22:05,156
and Psychic Killer with Jim Hutton , which was directed by her former husband ,

437
00:22:05,241 --> 00:22:06,699
actor-director Ray Danton .

438
00:22:06,867 --> 00:22:09,035
She was also in one of Elvis Presley 's movies ,

439
00:22:09,161 --> 00:22:11,037
the haunted-house comedy Tickle Me .

440
00:22:15,584 --> 00:22:16,835
In case you get confused in the scenes

441
00:22:16,919 --> 00:22:19,045
where Richard Carlson and Richard Denning go underwater ,

442
00:22:19,129 --> 00:22:20,672
if you can 't figure out which is which ,

443
00:22:20,756 --> 00:22:24,092
notice that Carlson has two tanks on his back , and Denning just one .

444
00:22:24,176 --> 00:22:27,679
They did that on purpose so people could tell ' em apart underwater .

445
00:22:27,763 --> 00:22:31,099
All the underwater scenes in Black Lagoon were shot in Florida by a second unit

446
00:22:31,183 --> 00:22:34,602
that was working there at the same time that Jack Arnold was shooting at Universal .

447
00:22:34,687 --> 00:22:37,605
Obviously , none of the actors could be there , they were busy at Universal .

448
00:22:37,773 --> 00:22:41,693
So any time the camera is underwater , know that those scenes were shot in Florida

449
00:22:41,861 --> 00:22:45,196
and that none of the people you 're seeing in those shots are the actual actors .

450
00:22:45,364 --> 00:22:48,741
They hired two college students to double for Richard Carlson and Richard Denning .

451
00:22:48,951 --> 00:22:51,202
A girl named Ginger Stanley doubled for Julie Adams ,

452
00:22:51,287 --> 00:22:53,705
and Ricou Browning , who I 'll talk about more later ,

453
00:22:53,873 --> 00:22:55,540
played The Gill Man .

454
00:22:55,666 --> 00:22:58,126
Today it 's easy to overlook what an amazing accomplishment

455
00:22:58,252 --> 00:23:01,129
Creature 's underwater photography was in 1953 .

456
00:23:01,213 --> 00:23:04,132
You have to keep in mind that 3-D was a fairly new process then ,

457
00:23:04,216 --> 00:23:06,134
and there were still a lot of bugs to iron out ,

458
00:23:06,343 --> 00:23:10,471
and now here 's Universal trying to figure out a way to take a 3-D camera underwater .

459
00:23:10,639 --> 00:23:13,349
I think that 's impressive enough , right there , but on top of that ,

460
00:23:13,475 --> 00:23:16,352
it had to be a portable motion-picture underwater camera ,

461
00:23:16,437 --> 00:23:18,688
which was practically unheard of at that time .

462
00:23:18,772 --> 00:23:23,234
This was 1953 , when virtually every other underwater scene in any movie to date

463
00:23:23,319 --> 00:23:26,487
had been photographed by stationary cameras , or through glass tanks .

464
00:23:26,822 --> 00:23:29,991
Universal shot some test footage with this portable underwater camera

465
00:23:30,159 --> 00:23:32,911
and they showed it to people from Life magazine , who were very impressed .

466
00:23:32,995 --> 00:23:35,663
And maybe that 's what gave someone at Universal the idea

467
00:23:35,789 --> 00:23:38,124
that a Life photographer named Peter Stackpole

468
00:23:38,208 --> 00:23:41,210
be asked to take a three-week leave of absence from Life magazine ,

469
00:23:41,378 --> 00:23:44,714
and go to Florida , and photograph the underwater scenes for the movie itself .

470
00:23:45,007 --> 00:23:48,760
Peter Stackpole was one of the four original photographers for Life magazine ,

471
00:23:48,844 --> 00:23:50,345
and he was one of their top photographers ,

472
00:23:50,471 --> 00:23:53,181
taking lots of photos of movie stars when he lived in Hollywood .

473
00:23:53,515 --> 00:23:56,309
He was also very enthusiastic about underwater photography ,

474
00:23:56,393 --> 00:23:59,771
he was an expert swimmer , and he had cameras equipped for underwater filming .

475
00:23:59,897 --> 00:24:03,524
Also in the back of Universal 's mind , of course , was the fact that his participation

476
00:24:03,609 --> 00:24:06,569
would practically ensure a layout on the movie in Life magazine ,

477
00:24:06,654 --> 00:24:08,863
and Universal would also get lots of first-rate stills

478
00:24:08,989 --> 00:24:11,032
of the underwater sequences for their own use .

479
00:24:11,408 --> 00:24:13,993
I don 't know if Stackpole turned them down , or what happened there ,

480
00:24:14,078 --> 00:24:16,871
but the guy Universal ended up hiring to direct the underwater sequences

481
00:24:16,997 --> 00:24:21,376
was a guy named James C. Havens , and that 's a funny story that I 'll save for later .

482
00:24:29,551 --> 00:24:33,304
The underwater scenes of Creature were shot at Wakulla Springs , Florida .

483
00:24:33,389 --> 00:24:35,723
Movies had been shot underwater there in the past .

484
00:24:35,808 --> 00:24:37,100
Some of the Tarzan pictures ,

485
00:24:37,184 --> 00:24:39,852
like Tarzan 's Secret Treasure from the old Johnny Weissmuller days .

486
00:24:40,062 --> 00:24:42,897
And the place was managed by a fellow named Newton Perry .

487
00:24:43,357 --> 00:24:45,942
Our first look at Ricou Browning as the underwater creature .

488
00:24:46,443 --> 00:24:50,238
Newton Perry was also involved with various Florida water shows and things like that ,

489
00:24:50,447 --> 00:24:52,198
and he was obviously known in Hollywood ,

490
00:24:52,282 --> 00:24:54,826
because when Universal decided to shoot part of Creature there ,

491
00:24:54,910 --> 00:24:56,244
he was the man they called .

492
00:24:56,412 --> 00:24:59,414
They asked Perry if he knew of any swimmers that he could recommend ,

493
00:24:59,498 --> 00:25:01,416
and they also asked if he knew of any locations

494
00:25:01,500 --> 00:25:04,419
where they might be able to find something that looked like the Black Lagoon .

495
00:25:04,503 --> 00:25:06,129
Perry said sure , he 'd show ' em around .

496
00:25:06,255 --> 00:25:08,965
But when the time came , he was off in Miami doing something ,

497
00:25:09,049 --> 00:25:10,466
and all of a sudden he wasn 't available .

498
00:25:10,592 --> 00:25:12,844
So Perry called his friend , Ricou Browning , in Tallahassee ,

499
00:25:12,928 --> 00:25:15,805
and asked Ricou to pick the Universal people up at the airport

500
00:25:15,889 --> 00:25:17,890
and take them down to the spring and show it to them .

501
00:25:18,058 --> 00:25:21,269
Ricou was 23 years old at the time , and attending Florida State .

502
00:25:21,437 --> 00:25:22,937
He 'd worked for Newton Perry in the past ,

503
00:25:23,063 --> 00:25:25,898
producing water shows at Weeki Wachee Springs .

504
00:25:26,025 --> 00:25:28,026
Ricou went to the airport and picked up Jack Arnold

505
00:25:28,110 --> 00:25:29,485
and a man named Scotty Welbourne ,

506
00:25:29,611 --> 00:25:32,321
who was going to be photographing the underwater scenes in Creature .

507
00:25:32,614 --> 00:25:35,950
Together , they went out to the spring , and Arnold and Welbourne liked it .

508
00:25:36,118 --> 00:25:38,036
Wakulla Springs is in the middle of nowhere .

509
00:25:38,120 --> 00:25:40,913
And in 1953 , and even right now , if you go there ,

510
00:25:40,998 --> 00:25:44,292
it would be like going to Florida with Ponce de León when he landed .

511
00:25:44,418 --> 00:25:46,044
It 's been that well protected .

512
00:25:46,128 --> 00:25:48,296
The bowl of the spring is about five-acres big .

513
00:25:48,380 --> 00:25:52,383
It 's the largest spring in the state of Florida , and it has the largest water flow .

514
00:25:52,468 --> 00:25:54,635
By the way , Ricou Browning was also a cave diver ,

515
00:25:54,720 --> 00:25:57,555
which means that he was hired at times to go down into underwater caves

516
00:25:57,639 --> 00:25:58,639
to map them out .

517
00:25:58,766 --> 00:26:02,977
The bottom of Wakulla Springs slopes down inside a cave , and it just keeps going .

518
00:26:03,103 --> 00:26:05,146
Ricou doesn 't know if anybody 's ever gone the distance

519
00:26:05,230 --> 00:26:07,148
as to how far you could actually go .

520
00:26:07,232 --> 00:26:09,734
The deepest that Ricou and the cave divers he used to work with

521
00:26:09,818 --> 00:26:14,655
ever went with air hoses there was , he says , 100 , at the most 110 , 120 feet .

522
00:26:14,865 --> 00:26:17,742
But , of course , they didn 't have some of the equipment , or the helium mixes

523
00:26:17,826 --> 00:26:19,243
that divers have nowadays ,

524
00:26:19,328 --> 00:26:22,080
so naturally they couldn 't go as deeply as divers today .

525
00:26:22,164 --> 00:26:25,958
Much more recently , divers equipped with this new gear and the new mixed gases

526
00:26:26,043 --> 00:26:29,003
have gone 200-300 feet down into Wakulla Springs .

527
00:26:29,171 --> 00:26:32,173
But , as Ricou said , " They still may not have found the bottom . "

528
00:26:32,508 --> 00:26:35,384
Anyway , Scotty Welbourne had a movie camera with him that day at Wakulla Springs ,

529
00:26:35,469 --> 00:26:37,929
and he said to Ricou , " Would you do me a favor ?

530
00:26:38,055 --> 00:26:40,681
" Would you swim in front of the camera so we can get some perspective

531
00:26:40,849 --> 00:26:44,769
" of the size of the logs , or the fish , or whatever with a human body ? "

532
00:26:44,853 --> 00:26:47,313
Ricou said , " Sure , " and he did swim for Welbourne .

533
00:26:47,397 --> 00:26:49,649
Arnold and Welbourne were very happy with the spring .

534
00:26:49,775 --> 00:26:51,484
And Ricou took ' em to the airport and they left .

535
00:26:51,568 --> 00:26:55,113
And , two or three weeks later , Ricou got a call from Jack Arnold , who said ,

536
00:26:55,280 --> 00:26:58,616
" We looked at the footage , we love the spring , and we love the way you swim .

537
00:26:58,700 --> 00:27:01,953
" I like your style . How would you like to be the Creature of the Black Lagoon ? "

538
00:27:02,037 --> 00:27:04,497
And Ricou said , " Fine . Let 's have at it . "

539
00:27:04,581 --> 00:27:07,458
Arnold said they 'd send him tickets so he could come out to Universal ,

540
00:27:07,543 --> 00:27:11,546
and they 'd build an outfit for him to wear . And the rest , as they say , is history .

541
00:27:11,630 --> 00:27:13,297
Come on , David . You can play house later .

542
00:27:13,382 --> 00:27:16,134
WEAVER : The script has Richard Denning say something obnoxious in every scene ,

543
00:27:16,218 --> 00:27:18,678
so that we never forget that he 's gonna be the heavy .

544
00:27:19,471 --> 00:27:21,806
Browning went out to California and reported to Universal ,

545
00:27:21,890 --> 00:27:24,308
where various outfits for The Gill Man were being devised .

546
00:27:24,518 --> 00:27:27,812
I should interrupt myself here and mention that William Alland 's concept of The Gill Man

547
00:27:27,938 --> 00:27:30,273
was much more human than The Gill Man we see in the movie .

548
00:27:30,357 --> 00:27:33,818
Alland had a sculptor sculpt the head of a very sad , beautiful monster .

549
00:27:33,902 --> 00:27:37,071
In fact , he told me , " It wasn 't a monster . It was far more attractive ,

550
00:27:37,156 --> 00:27:39,991
" more romantic-looking than the beast they ended up with .

551
00:27:40,075 --> 00:27:43,035
" It had fish lips and gills and a vaguely fishlike face ,

552
00:27:43,120 --> 00:27:45,121
" sort of an aquatic development of a man . "

553
00:27:45,330 --> 00:27:47,665
Alland told me , " It would still frighten you ,

554
00:27:47,749 --> 00:27:51,085
" but it would frighten you because of how human it was , not the other way around . "

555
00:27:51,170 --> 00:27:54,547
And the studio said , " Get outta here , that 's not scary enough . "

556
00:27:54,673 --> 00:27:57,341
That 's when the Universal makeup department took over .

557
00:27:58,343 --> 00:28:00,011
Kay doesn 't know there 's a creature in the Amazon ,

558
00:28:00,095 --> 00:28:03,222
but she does know from Lucas that it 's full of nine-foot killer catfish .

559
00:28:03,307 --> 00:28:05,516
You couldn 't get me in that lagoon with dynamite .

560
00:28:05,934 --> 00:28:08,019
Here comes the best and the most famous scene in the movie ,

561
00:28:08,103 --> 00:28:09,770
as Julie Adams takes a dip in the lagoon

562
00:28:09,855 --> 00:28:12,440
and is ogled from below by yet another male admirer ,

563
00:28:12,524 --> 00:28:13,691
The Gill Man .

564
00:28:13,775 --> 00:28:16,152
Julie Adams is doubled in this scene by Ginger Stanley ,

565
00:28:16,403 --> 00:28:19,238
a " mermaid " swimmer at Weeki Wachee Spring .

566
00:28:19,948 --> 00:28:23,993
When the camera is underwater , it 's Florida cinematographer Scotty Welbourne 's camera ,

567
00:28:24,077 --> 00:28:25,703
and you 're looking at Ginger Stanley .

568
00:28:25,787 --> 00:28:29,165
When the camera is above water , then it 's cinematographer William Snyder

569
00:28:29,249 --> 00:28:31,959
photographing Julie Adams in Park Lake at Universal .

570
00:28:32,294 --> 00:28:34,378
Ricou Browning 's swimming performance in this scene

571
00:28:34,463 --> 00:28:36,964
is the poetic / dramatic highlight of the movie .

572
00:28:37,299 --> 00:28:39,717
The thing that makes the creature as image and myth

573
00:28:39,927 --> 00:28:43,179
are the wonderful , dynamic , swimming Ricou shots you 're about to see .

574
00:28:44,139 --> 00:28:47,016
There 'll be no dialogue for the next few minutes , so now might be a good time

575
00:28:47,100 --> 00:28:49,560
to talk a little about the music of Creature from the Black Lagoon .

576
00:28:49,811 --> 00:28:52,188
Sci-fi movie music expert David Schecter ,

577
00:28:52,356 --> 00:28:55,066
who releases classic SF film scores on CD

578
00:28:55,150 --> 00:28:59,237
under the umbrella title Monstrous Movie Music , calls the Creature score

579
00:28:59,446 --> 00:29:03,241
" one of the most varied and memorable in all of ' 50s sci-fi horrordom . "

580
00:29:03,450 --> 00:29:05,451
" Part of the experience of watching a creature film

581
00:29:05,535 --> 00:29:08,204
" is the considerable musical accompaniment . "

582
00:29:08,497 --> 00:29:11,999
And he 's right . A hefty 63 % of Creature has background music .

583
00:29:12,334 --> 00:29:15,002
Of the 44 cues listed on Creature 's cue sheets ,

584
00:29:15,170 --> 00:29:17,296
29 were composed specifically for the movie ,

585
00:29:17,381 --> 00:29:20,508
with the other 15 lifted from prior Universal pictures .

586
00:29:20,759 --> 00:29:23,219
The 29 originals were , according to Schecter ,

587
00:29:23,303 --> 00:29:26,931
composed by Hans J. Salter , 12 , Henry Mancini , 10 ,

588
00:29:27,015 --> 00:29:31,018
and Herman Stein , seven . The music we 're hearing right now is by Herman Stein ,

589
00:29:31,103 --> 00:29:33,104
and the cue is called Kay and the Monster .

590
00:29:33,188 --> 00:29:36,107
" The older films that contributed library music to Creature 's score

591
00:29:36,275 --> 00:29:37,692
" were from many different genres , "

592
00:29:37,776 --> 00:29:41,153
writes Schecter . " Some , like Mr. Peabody and the Mermaid ,

593
00:29:41,238 --> 00:29:42,571
" were water-themed pictures ,

594
00:29:42,656 --> 00:29:45,700
" which is why the music was considered for use in Creature from the Black Lagoon .

595
00:29:45,784 --> 00:29:47,535
" And also why it was so well-suited .

596
00:29:47,619 --> 00:29:49,912
" Westerns also played a role in Creature 's score ,

597
00:29:50,038 --> 00:29:52,915
" as they did in other Universal sci-fi scores of the ' 50s .

598
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:57,003
" But what sets Creature 's score apart from that of many other Universal sci-fi films

599
00:29:57,087 --> 00:29:59,714
" is its reliance upon a single musical motif ,

600
00:29:59,881 --> 00:30:02,466
" Herman Stein 's memorable three-note creature theme .

601
00:30:02,551 --> 00:30:03,884
( TRUMPETS PLAYING THREE-NOTE THEME )

602
00:30:03,969 --> 00:30:06,387
WEAVER : " In the film , practically every time you see the creature ,

603
00:30:06,471 --> 00:30:08,264
" or his hands , or his back , or his feet ,

604
00:30:08,432 --> 00:30:11,392
" or even his wet footprints , you hear his three-note theme ,

605
00:30:11,476 --> 00:30:14,395
" often played on flutter-tongued trumpets . "

606
00:30:14,646 --> 00:30:18,316
Schecter calls the three-note Creature theme , " a memorable musical signature , "

607
00:30:18,400 --> 00:30:19,400
which it certainly is .

608
00:30:19,484 --> 00:30:23,654
It 's instantly recognizable , even when it crops up in non-Universal sci-fi movies ,

609
00:30:23,739 --> 00:30:26,866
like Women of the Prehistoric Planet and Dracula vs. Frankenstein

610
00:30:26,950 --> 00:30:29,327
that lifted music from Creature .

611
00:30:32,998 --> 00:30:35,041
People who write about this movie often speculate

612
00:30:35,167 --> 00:30:37,501
that this scene here was an inspiration to Steven Spielberg

613
00:30:37,586 --> 00:30:39,503
when he shot some of the scenes of Jaws .

614
00:30:39,755 --> 00:30:43,174
Because the plot of Creature is so indebted to King Kong , I think back to Kong .

615
00:30:43,258 --> 00:30:47,011
This scene of a curious Gill Man playfully poking and touching her legs

616
00:30:47,220 --> 00:30:48,471
makes me think of the scene in Kong ,

617
00:30:48,555 --> 00:30:50,556
where King Kong takes Fay Wray up to the mountain top ,

618
00:30:50,640 --> 00:30:52,516
and pokes her and tickles her .

619
00:30:52,684 --> 00:30:56,020
I also wanna talk briefly about some of the people who worked on the Creature costume .

620
00:30:56,104 --> 00:31:00,024
Prominent among them was a creative designer named Milicent Patrick .

621
00:31:00,108 --> 00:31:03,402
Talk about Beauty and the Beast . She was born an Italian baroness ,

622
00:31:03,695 --> 00:31:07,365
she was a former Goldwyn Girl , a model , an interior decorator , an artist .

623
00:31:07,449 --> 00:31:10,326
She was the first woman animator for Walt Disney Studios ,

624
00:31:10,452 --> 00:31:14,038
and she helped design the creature and did some of the final painting on the costume .

625
00:31:14,122 --> 00:31:15,664
Chris Mueller was one of the sculptors ,

626
00:31:15,749 --> 00:31:18,292
Tom Case was a painter on the various Gill Man suits ,

627
00:31:18,377 --> 00:31:21,253
and Jack Kevan , who was Bud Westmore 's right-hand man ...

628
00:31:21,421 --> 00:31:23,756
He was Bud Westmore 's right and left-hand man .

629
00:31:24,091 --> 00:31:26,008
Jack Kevan did a little of everything .

630
00:31:26,134 --> 00:31:29,303
Kevan worked as Westmore 's assistant , but he was a hell of a lot more than that .

631
00:31:29,471 --> 00:31:33,391
He was a real hands-on guy who did most of whatever work had to be done .

632
00:31:33,475 --> 00:31:35,976
He was born in Pittsburgh , attended UCLA ,

633
00:31:36,269 --> 00:31:39,480
and he went to work doing makeup at MGM in 1932 .

634
00:31:39,606 --> 00:31:43,150
He made up the main characters in The Wizard of Oz , and then , during World War ll ,

635
00:31:43,318 --> 00:31:46,904
he made prosthetic appliances for badly wounded men throughout the Pacific .

636
00:31:47,072 --> 00:31:48,823
After the war , he got a job at Universal ,

637
00:31:48,949 --> 00:31:52,451
where he worked on A and C Meet Frankenstein , It Came from Outer Space ,

638
00:31:52,661 --> 00:31:55,538
Man of a Thousand Faces , Tarantula , scores of movies .

639
00:31:55,705 --> 00:31:59,458
And in 1958 he and Creature 's dialogue director , Irvin Berwick ,

640
00:31:59,668 --> 00:32:01,544
struck out on their own and made a low-budget horror film

641
00:32:01,628 --> 00:32:03,587
called The Monster of Piedras Blancas ,

642
00:32:03,672 --> 00:32:06,507
a thinly-disguised attempt to duplicate the success of Creature

643
00:32:06,633 --> 00:32:07,883
with a lookalike sea monster .

644
00:32:08,510 --> 00:32:09,802
Jack Kevan told me ,

645
00:32:09,886 --> 00:32:12,012
" They were lucky in finding a person like Ricou Browning ,

646
00:32:12,139 --> 00:32:13,431
" a person who could swim underwater ,

647
00:32:13,515 --> 00:32:16,142
" and not be restricted by all the equipment he had to wear .

648
00:32:16,226 --> 00:32:20,187
" We took a full body cast of him . On top of his figure , we built the monster to fit him ,

649
00:32:20,272 --> 00:32:23,649
" mold wise , in several pieces . I can 't tell you how many now ,

650
00:32:23,733 --> 00:32:26,777
" but I would guess between six and ten . They were cast in foam rubber ,

651
00:32:26,862 --> 00:32:31,365
" which at the time was something new . The costume fit him pretty skintight . "

652
00:32:31,450 --> 00:32:35,536
It sure did . Ben Chapman , the 6 ' 5 " actor who played the creature at Universal ,

653
00:32:35,745 --> 00:32:39,248
describes The Gill Man suit as fitting as closely as an outer layer of skin .

654
00:32:39,749 --> 00:32:42,209
Luckily , neither Ricou nor Ben was claustrophobic .

655
00:32:42,335 --> 00:32:45,463
Because anybody who gets into a suit like that and finds out they are claustrophobic

656
00:32:45,547 --> 00:32:46,797
is in a world of trouble .

657
00:32:46,882 --> 00:32:47,965
Once you were in that suit ,

658
00:32:48,049 --> 00:32:51,719
your eyeballs and the inside of your mouth was all that was showing .

659
00:32:51,970 --> 00:32:55,222
Unfortunately , nothing is left of any of The Gill Man costumes today .

660
00:32:55,307 --> 00:32:57,766
They were made of foam rubber , and that stuff doesn 't last .

661
00:32:57,851 --> 00:33:00,311
Even the molds used to make the suits have been destroyed .

662
00:33:00,395 --> 00:33:03,772
Universal threw them away years ago to make some room .

663
00:33:06,318 --> 00:33:09,653
It 's a small thing , but I was always bothered by the fact that they want us to believe

664
00:33:09,738 --> 00:33:12,448
the creature was able to crack the boom just by his powerful swimming .

665
00:33:12,908 --> 00:33:14,241
For one thing , the boat 's not even anchored ,

666
00:33:14,451 --> 00:33:16,869
the creature would just tow the boat around the lagoon .

667
00:33:16,995 --> 00:33:20,122
Frank Lovejoy was originally considered for the starring role in Creature .

668
00:33:20,207 --> 00:33:23,167
Lovejoy was the police lieutenant hero of Warner Bros. ' House of Wax ,

669
00:33:23,335 --> 00:33:25,127
a huge 3D hit earlier that year ,

670
00:33:25,378 --> 00:33:28,214
and maybe that was part of the reason Universal thought of him for Creature .

671
00:33:28,298 --> 00:33:30,758
But then they decided to go with Richard Carlson instead .

672
00:33:30,842 --> 00:33:34,261
For the part of Dr. Maia , Universal first thought of Ramon Novarro .

673
00:33:34,471 --> 00:33:36,764
Ramon Novarro was a Mexican-born leading man

674
00:33:36,890 --> 00:33:39,975
who became a big , Rudolph-Valentino-like star in the silent era .

675
00:33:40,227 --> 00:33:44,188
He had the title role in one of the greatest silent epics , MGM 's Ben Hur .

676
00:33:44,356 --> 00:33:47,441
But instead of Novarro , Universal went with Antonio Moreno ,

677
00:33:47,609 --> 00:33:51,153
who back in silent days was kind of like the poor man 's Ramon Novarro .

678
00:33:51,321 --> 00:33:55,574
Creature was one of Moreno 's final screen roles . He died rich in 1967 .

679
00:33:56,785 --> 00:33:59,453
This was strange at a movie company famous for its monsters ,

680
00:33:59,538 --> 00:34:02,790
but Universal tried to avoid putting an exploitative title on the movie .

681
00:34:02,874 --> 00:34:05,918
They called it , simply , Black Lagoon for a long stretch of time ,

682
00:34:06,002 --> 00:34:08,295
but then they decided that that just wasn 't going to cut it

683
00:34:08,380 --> 00:34:11,298
and they sent out a memo requesting submission of alternate titles .

684
00:34:11,466 --> 00:34:14,635
The memo read in part , " The request is for a title that may better suggest

685
00:34:14,719 --> 00:34:19,557
" the shock and mystery value , and perhaps the prehistoric implications of the story .

686
00:34:19,641 --> 00:34:22,893
" However , we have been asked to stay away from words like ' monster ' or ' beast

687
00:34:22,978 --> 00:34:26,188
" to avoid a title that might downgrade the picture . "

688
00:34:26,273 --> 00:34:28,148
Somebody suggested The Web and the Claw ,

689
00:34:28,233 --> 00:34:31,735
and William Alland , who had a lot of success with It Came from Outer Space ,

690
00:34:31,820 --> 00:34:34,154
recommended It Came From Out of the Sea .

691
00:34:34,239 --> 00:34:37,825
The other four titles he suggested were The Demon of the Deep , The Sea Demon ,

692
00:34:37,993 --> 00:34:40,786
The River of Terror , and It Walks the Sea .

693
00:34:41,079 --> 00:34:43,664
Incidentally , when Alland heard in the 1980s

694
00:34:43,748 --> 00:34:45,833
that Universal was thinking of remaking Creature ,

695
00:34:45,959 --> 00:34:49,378
he felt that the studio should do another sequel instead of a remake .

696
00:34:49,462 --> 00:34:52,590
Since the creature that Alland had originally envisioned was so romantic ,

697
00:34:52,799 --> 00:34:56,176
he thought it should have a family , a mate and one or two little ones .

698
00:34:56,261 --> 00:34:59,888
In Alland 's story they were all removed from the Black Lagoon and transported to a lake

699
00:34:59,973 --> 00:35:02,766
on the estate of a very wealthy family in the US .

700
00:35:02,851 --> 00:35:05,853
They can communicate with the family and a whole relationship develops ,

701
00:35:06,021 --> 00:35:08,147
but unfortunately some heavies find out about them

702
00:35:08,231 --> 00:35:10,024
and capture one or both of the children ,

703
00:35:10,108 --> 00:35:12,234
and the mother and father have to go out after them .

704
00:35:12,360 --> 00:35:15,404
Alland wrote it all up and tried to make himself send it in to Universal

705
00:35:15,488 --> 00:35:19,283
during all the many years that the studio talked about doing a new Creature movie ,

706
00:35:19,367 --> 00:35:21,368
but he never could bring himself to submit it .

707
00:35:21,494 --> 00:35:23,746
William Alland passed away in 1997 .

708
00:35:24,664 --> 00:35:26,624
A second unit director named James C. Havens

709
00:35:26,708 --> 00:35:29,335
was hired to direct the underwater second unit stuff in Creature .

710
00:35:29,502 --> 00:35:31,879
And I 'm sure Universal felt very fortunate to have gotten him

711
00:35:31,963 --> 00:35:33,881
and no doubt had a lot of confidence in him .

712
00:35:34,049 --> 00:35:35,758
He 'd been around the movie business most of his life ,

713
00:35:35,842 --> 00:35:38,427
and he 'd been around boats and water practically all his life .

714
00:35:38,678 --> 00:35:41,889
I tried to track him down several years ago and I finally found his phone number ,

715
00:35:42,098 --> 00:35:45,851
but when I called , his wife answered and told me he had died several years before .

716
00:35:45,935 --> 00:35:49,563
But I had a nice conversation with her , because she had married him in 1924 .

717
00:35:49,648 --> 00:35:51,982
And they 'd been married for 65-and-a-half years ,

718
00:35:52,067 --> 00:35:54,360
so I figured she might know a little something about him .

719
00:35:54,694 --> 00:35:56,612
And she made him sound like a real interesting guy .

720
00:35:56,863 --> 00:35:59,823
He started out as an art director at MGM around 1924 ,

721
00:35:59,908 --> 00:36:03,410
the same year he got married , and gradually , he got into marine directing ,

722
00:36:03,495 --> 00:36:06,413
which means he directed the seagoing scenes for movies , big movies .

723
00:36:06,498 --> 00:36:08,290
Like Mutiny on the Bounty with Clark Gable ,

724
00:36:08,375 --> 00:36:11,293
and Captains Courageous with Spencer Tracy , and pictures like that .

725
00:36:11,378 --> 00:36:15,839
And Havens always owned a boat . He had a sailboat first , without any engine ,

726
00:36:15,924 --> 00:36:19,426
and then he and his wife had ketches and motorboats and all kinds of boats .

727
00:36:19,511 --> 00:36:21,679
Then he was a captain in the Marines during World War II ,

728
00:36:21,763 --> 00:36:23,430
a demolition expert for the Marines .

729
00:36:23,515 --> 00:36:26,433
And when John Ford made the movie Mogambo in East Africa ,

730
00:36:26,518 --> 00:36:29,520
Havens shot the animal scenes for Mogambo in Tanganyika .

731
00:36:29,604 --> 00:36:31,689
And he went around scouting locations in a Land Rover

732
00:36:31,773 --> 00:36:33,857
right during the big Mau Mau Rebellion .

733
00:36:33,942 --> 00:36:35,442
She made him sound like a real man 's man ,

734
00:36:35,527 --> 00:36:37,861
a guy who could handle anything that could possibly come up ,

735
00:36:37,946 --> 00:36:39,446
which I 'm sure he was .

736
00:36:39,531 --> 00:36:41,240
So it was so funny , to me anyway ,

737
00:36:41,324 --> 00:36:43,909
to hear what happened to him during Creature from the Black Lagoon .

738
00:36:43,993 --> 00:36:46,412
Havens goes to Florida to direct the underwater scenes

739
00:36:46,496 --> 00:36:48,539
and Scotty Welbourne is there to photograph ' em .

740
00:36:48,623 --> 00:36:51,500
And a few days pass , and William Alland gets a phone call from Florida

741
00:36:51,584 --> 00:36:52,876
from the production manager ,

742
00:36:52,961 --> 00:36:55,921
who tells him , " You 'd better come down here , there 's all hell breaking loose .

743
00:36:56,005 --> 00:36:58,632
" Havens and Welbourne , they 're not getting along . "

744
00:36:58,842 --> 00:37:02,302
Anyway , Alland hears that all hell is breaking loose , so he goes to Florida .

745
00:37:02,512 --> 00:37:05,681
And what 's happening is Havens is afraid to go underwater .

746
00:37:06,099 --> 00:37:07,224
Hang on for a second .

747
00:37:07,308 --> 00:37:10,185
This is sort of an odd shot here , this very blurry shot of a hand ,

748
00:37:10,270 --> 00:37:12,312
followed a few seconds later by a shot of The Gill Man

749
00:37:12,397 --> 00:37:14,815
swimming away in another hard-to-see shot .

750
00:37:15,275 --> 00:37:16,442
Seems to me like shots like these

751
00:37:16,526 --> 00:37:18,736
should have preceded our first good look at The Gill Man .

752
00:37:18,820 --> 00:37:20,446
They 'd have made great teaser shots .

753
00:37:21,114 --> 00:37:24,366
Anyway , I asked Mrs. Havens if her husband liked to dive , and she said , " No , no .

754
00:37:24,451 --> 00:37:28,704
" He didn 't like going under the water at all . " So , in Florida , during the making of Creature ,

755
00:37:28,788 --> 00:37:31,248
the best Havens could do was float on the surface of the water

756
00:37:31,332 --> 00:37:32,833
with a face mask and a snorkel ,

757
00:37:33,001 --> 00:37:37,629
and look down 50 feet or 60 feet or whatever , and try to figure out what was going on .

758
00:37:37,756 --> 00:37:40,340
He had sketched out the way he wanted each shot to be shot ,

759
00:37:40,425 --> 00:37:42,926
but Scotty Welbourne said he couldn 't work that way .

760
00:37:43,011 --> 00:37:46,597
Havens , floating on the surface of the water , couldn 't see what was at the bottom ,

761
00:37:46,681 --> 00:37:49,016
and I guess he was sketching things that weren 't even down there ,

762
00:37:49,184 --> 00:37:51,685
and Welbourne was saying that all he , Welbourne , could do

763
00:37:51,770 --> 00:37:54,146
was take advantage of what was down there at the bottom .

764
00:37:54,522 --> 00:37:57,733
So Bill Alland 's idea was , he 'd put on scuba gear and he 'd go down ,

765
00:37:57,859 --> 00:38:00,152
and maybe that would kind of shame Havens into doing it .

766
00:38:00,695 --> 00:38:03,071
So Alland put on the equipment and he dove down 50 feet ,

767
00:38:03,156 --> 00:38:06,158
where they had underwater light platforms and all kinds of stuff set up ,

768
00:38:06,242 --> 00:38:08,452
and he watched Scotty Welbourne doing his thing down there .

769
00:38:08,787 --> 00:38:10,412
Then Alland came back up and he told Havens ,

770
00:38:10,497 --> 00:38:13,373
" Look , either you go down there , and see what he 's doing and work with him ,

771
00:38:13,458 --> 00:38:15,042
" or you 've just got to get out of the way . "

772
00:38:15,210 --> 00:38:18,003
And Havens said , " Okay , fine , " he 'd just get out of the way .

773
00:38:18,546 --> 00:38:21,340
So , according to William Alland , even though in the opening credits , it says ,

774
00:38:21,424 --> 00:38:24,092
" Underwater sequences directed by James C. Havens , "

775
00:38:24,219 --> 00:38:26,512
Alland says , " He did not have a damn thing to do

776
00:38:26,596 --> 00:38:28,639
" with any of the stuff that went on underwater .

777
00:38:28,723 --> 00:38:31,016
" Scotty Welbourne directed the underwater stuff . "

778
00:38:32,060 --> 00:38:34,686
Ricou Browning also remembers the situation with Havens ,

779
00:38:34,771 --> 00:38:37,314
but doesn 't tell the story the same way Alland did .

780
00:38:37,398 --> 00:38:39,858
Ricou said it wasn 't that Havens was afraid to go underwater ,

781
00:38:39,943 --> 00:38:41,193
it 's just that he wasn 't capable ,

782
00:38:41,277 --> 00:38:43,695
in other words , he wasn 't trained to go underwater .

783
00:38:43,780 --> 00:38:48,033
According to Ricou , " Havens got in an inner tube and put on a face mask

784
00:38:48,117 --> 00:38:50,828
" and he would watch , as much as he could , what was going on .

785
00:38:50,912 --> 00:38:52,913
" He depended a great deal on Scotty Welbourne ,

786
00:38:52,997 --> 00:38:55,123
" and Scotty was a pretty sharp character .

787
00:38:55,208 --> 00:38:58,418
" And Havens just kind of left it to him and us to do our thing .

788
00:38:58,503 --> 00:39:01,255
" He would watch , and comment now and then . "

789
00:39:02,298 --> 00:39:06,051
I asked Ricou , I said , " In a perfect world , should the credits of Creature say ,

790
00:39:06,135 --> 00:39:08,220
" ' Underwater scenes directed by James Havens , '

791
00:39:08,304 --> 00:39:10,848
" or ' Underwater scenes directed by Scotty Welbourne ? " '

792
00:39:10,932 --> 00:39:13,851
And he answered , " I would just say a combination of people .

793
00:39:13,935 --> 00:39:16,144
" I would give Havens credit . "

794
00:39:17,146 --> 00:39:19,022
And the guy who filmed the out-of-water scenes in Creature ,

795
00:39:19,107 --> 00:39:21,775
he was no slouch either . He was a guy named William Snyder ,

796
00:39:21,860 --> 00:39:24,570
who 'd been around for years , and had photographed a lot of pictures

797
00:39:24,654 --> 00:39:27,489
and had received three Oscar nominations in the 1940s .

798
00:39:27,699 --> 00:39:29,074
Aloma of the South Seas ,

799
00:39:29,158 --> 00:39:31,702
The Loves of Carmen and Jolson Sings Again .

800
00:39:31,995 --> 00:39:34,705
After Creature , he worked a lot at Walt Disney Studios .

801
00:39:34,789 --> 00:39:38,208
He also shot The Conqueror , the John Wayne Genghis Khan movie ,

802
00:39:38,293 --> 00:39:41,086
a movie that 's notorious , A , for not being very good ,

803
00:39:41,170 --> 00:39:45,090
and , B , for having been shot on location in Utah near an atomic test site .

804
00:39:45,174 --> 00:39:49,177
A frighteningly high percentage of the cast and crew were later stricken by cancer .

805
00:39:49,262 --> 00:39:52,472
Welbourne 's no longer with us , and maybe that had something to do with it .

806
00:39:52,599 --> 00:39:54,099
But he 'd be 100-and-something years old today ,

807
00:39:54,183 --> 00:39:55,517
and maybe that had something to do with it , too .

808
00:39:55,643 --> 00:39:56,643
... it 's the greatest find yet .

809
00:39:56,728 --> 00:39:57,978
Nothing compares to it .

810
00:39:58,062 --> 00:40:00,022
I 've got the proof here in my lens .

811
00:40:05,528 --> 00:40:07,446
WEAVER : Richard Carlson developing the photograph here

812
00:40:07,530 --> 00:40:10,073
makes me think back to the Gabriel Figueroa story ,

813
00:40:10,158 --> 00:40:13,911
Figueroa saying that he had once seen a photo of the real-life Amazon monster ,

814
00:40:13,995 --> 00:40:15,704
and that he could get a print of that photograph

815
00:40:15,788 --> 00:40:17,664
if Orson Welles and his friends didn 't believe him .

816
00:40:18,458 --> 00:40:20,083
Ben Chapman will be making another appearance

817
00:40:20,168 --> 00:40:21,668
as the creature in a minute or so .

818
00:40:21,920 --> 00:40:24,463
Ben Chapman is a Tahitian , born in Oakland , California ,

819
00:40:24,547 --> 00:40:26,965
while his parents were on a trip to the United States .

820
00:40:27,342 --> 00:40:30,886
He was raised in Tahiti , relocated to the US in 1940 ,

821
00:40:30,970 --> 00:40:33,680
and went to school in the Bay Area of San Francisco .

822
00:40:33,765 --> 00:40:37,351
He worked as a Tahitian dancer in nightclubs before he landed his first movie job ,

823
00:40:37,685 --> 00:40:41,813
a bit in an MGM musical with a Tahitian setting called Pagan Love Song .

824
00:40:41,898 --> 00:40:44,775
He was a dancer in it , but he got along so well with the director

825
00:40:44,859 --> 00:40:48,445
that he also got an additional little part as Rita Moreno 's boyfriend .

826
00:40:48,529 --> 00:40:49,780
He had other small parts like that

827
00:40:49,864 --> 00:40:52,324
before the Korean War sidetracked his movie career .

828
00:40:52,408 --> 00:40:56,703
He served in Korea , and upon his return , he was hired as a stock player at Universal .

829
00:40:57,038 --> 00:40:58,956
Creature was the best movie role he ever had ,

830
00:40:59,040 --> 00:41:01,708
even if all you ever see of him is the inside of his mouth ,

831
00:41:02,001 --> 00:41:03,293
but he kept plugging away for years ,

832
00:41:03,378 --> 00:41:06,380
acting on TV and Jungle Jim movies , and things like that .

833
00:41:06,464 --> 00:41:09,675
Eventually , he moved to Hawaii and got a job with a realty company ,

834
00:41:09,759 --> 00:41:11,718
but his business card had artwork of the creature on it .

835
00:41:11,803 --> 00:41:13,261
For a merman , he takes a fine picture .

836
00:41:13,846 --> 00:41:16,181
This wasn 't imagination , Doctor .

837
00:41:16,265 --> 00:41:17,349
I 'm sorry , David .

838
00:41:17,433 --> 00:41:20,227
What proof do we have if we never find it again ?

839
00:41:21,020 --> 00:41:23,772
WEAVER : Chapman plays The Gill Man in the scenes shot at Universal .

840
00:41:23,856 --> 00:41:26,274
And he says that Jack Arnold insisted he walk like this .

841
00:41:26,567 --> 00:41:27,776
The creature glides in water ,

842
00:41:27,860 --> 00:41:30,821
so Arnold wanted him to glide on land , too , to shuffle like that .

843
00:41:31,155 --> 00:41:34,324
In fact , they sometimes put some lead , maybe as much as 10 pounds of it ,

844
00:41:34,409 --> 00:41:35,909
in the bottoms of his creature boots ,

845
00:41:35,994 --> 00:41:38,412
a reminder for Chapman not to lift his feet when he walked .

846
00:41:39,330 --> 00:41:42,124
I once had a very strange conversation with Henry Escalante ,

847
00:41:42,208 --> 00:41:43,667
the actor who just got attacked .

848
00:41:43,751 --> 00:41:47,462
I located him and called him up , fishing , as it were , for more Creature stories ,

849
00:41:47,672 --> 00:41:51,008
and very , very casually , he told me that he played the creature .

850
00:41:51,259 --> 00:41:54,803
He talked about how hot the suit was and how tough it was to breathe sometimes .

851
00:41:54,887 --> 00:41:57,347
He told me he had a guy with a fan fanning him all the time .

852
00:41:57,432 --> 00:41:59,349
He talked about how much he sweated in the costume

853
00:41:59,434 --> 00:42:02,227
and how much the perspiration burned when it got into his eyes .

854
00:42:02,603 --> 00:42:04,479
He talked about how he got along with Jack Arnold .

855
00:42:04,564 --> 00:42:06,273
He could not have sounded more casual .

856
00:42:06,441 --> 00:42:08,358
He sounded absolutely believable ,

857
00:42:08,443 --> 00:42:10,777
except there wasn 't one word of truth in anything he said .

858
00:42:11,112 --> 00:42:14,614
It was probably the strangest conversation I ever had with anybody about a movie .

859
00:42:15,033 --> 00:42:16,033
DR. MAIA : Quickly !

860
00:42:16,993 --> 00:42:19,703
WEAVER : By the way , you 'll notice that these footprints they 're about to show

861
00:42:19,787 --> 00:42:21,496
don 't match the way the creature was walking .

862
00:42:21,581 --> 00:42:23,665
He dragged his feet , he didn 't take those kinds of steps .

863
00:42:24,959 --> 00:42:27,836
Vision was a problem for both Ben Chapman and Ricou Browning .

864
00:42:27,920 --> 00:42:30,630
For Chapman , they made different sets of eyes for different situations .

865
00:42:30,715 --> 00:42:33,341
It all depended on how far away from the camera he was .

866
00:42:33,509 --> 00:42:36,219
For close-ups of the creature , they gave him eyes that were complete ,

867
00:42:36,304 --> 00:42:39,264
that filled up the whole eye socket , and he could hardly see through those .

868
00:42:39,432 --> 00:42:40,891
Somebody would have to use a flashlight

869
00:42:40,975 --> 00:42:42,976
and point it in the direction they wanted him to walk ,

870
00:42:43,061 --> 00:42:46,813
and Chapman , who could just barely see the light , would head off that way .

871
00:42:46,981 --> 00:42:49,316
For medium shots , the holes in the eyes were a little bigger

872
00:42:49,400 --> 00:42:50,567
and he could see a little better ,

873
00:42:50,651 --> 00:42:53,278
and for long shots , they just gave him eyes with good-size holes in them ,

874
00:42:53,362 --> 00:42:54,529
and he could see just fine .

875
00:42:55,114 --> 00:42:57,783
For Ricou Browning , working underwater , it was a lot harder .

876
00:42:58,034 --> 00:43:00,744
At first , he tried to wear little goggles under the creature mask ,

877
00:43:00,912 --> 00:43:03,330
but once water got in the goggles , you couldn 't get it out .

878
00:43:03,664 --> 00:43:05,415
They thought about giving him a facemask ,

879
00:43:05,500 --> 00:43:08,085
but that made The Gill Man face bulge out too far .

880
00:43:08,377 --> 00:43:10,796
Finally , they just gave up , and he used his naked eyes .

881
00:43:11,214 --> 00:43:12,422
But that was tough , because I guess

882
00:43:12,507 --> 00:43:14,758
the creature mask was just a little bigger than his head ,

883
00:43:14,842 --> 00:43:18,011
and sometimes the eyeholes were a couple of inches from his real eyes .

884
00:43:18,554 --> 00:43:19,846
So seeing out the eyeholes

885
00:43:19,931 --> 00:43:23,308
was kind of like trying to look through a keyhole from a couple inches away .

886
00:43:23,392 --> 00:43:27,104
He said his vision was often very blurred , and that a lot of it was kind of hit and miss .

887
00:43:28,689 --> 00:43:30,315
By the way , the creature suit we see in the movie

888
00:43:30,399 --> 00:43:33,360
was not the first creature suit that the makeup department came up with .

889
00:43:33,444 --> 00:43:36,404
The first one they devised was very different from the eventual design ,

890
00:43:36,489 --> 00:43:39,282
and it was rejected pretty quickly . Ricou Browning described it

891
00:43:39,367 --> 00:43:42,119
as more streamlined than the creature suit we know today .

892
00:43:42,286 --> 00:43:45,330
According to Ricou , " It had less scales , less fins ,

893
00:43:45,414 --> 00:43:48,708
" and the head was more like you were wearing a tight stocking over your face .

894
00:43:48,793 --> 00:43:51,169
" A little bit more human in appearance . "

895
00:43:51,254 --> 00:43:53,964
That early , rejected head was sculpted by Chris Mueller ,

896
00:43:54,048 --> 00:43:56,800
almost certainly from a design by Milicent Patrick .

897
00:43:56,884 --> 00:43:58,135
And as I mentioned earlier ,

898
00:43:58,219 --> 00:44:01,054
when Universal started thinking about making a second Creature movie ,

899
00:44:01,222 --> 00:44:03,181
there was talk of adding a Gill Woman .

900
00:44:03,266 --> 00:44:06,059
The rejected Gill Man head , or something very much like it ,

901
00:44:06,144 --> 00:44:08,812
would have been used as the head of the female creature .

902
00:44:08,896 --> 00:44:12,315
When the rejected head was originally made , it was supposed to be a Gill Man ,

903
00:44:12,400 --> 00:44:16,236
but the eventual Gill Man head used in the picture looked so ferocious

904
00:44:16,320 --> 00:44:19,823
that this more human head ended up looked almost feminine by comparison .

905
00:44:25,830 --> 00:44:29,166
Q Magazine : " This horrendous pseudo-science-fiction melodrama

906
00:44:29,250 --> 00:44:33,461
" revolves , rather wildly , in three dimensions and with considerable excitement ,

907
00:44:33,546 --> 00:44:37,215
" around a poor ancestral fish that never quite made the grade to man .

908
00:44:37,300 --> 00:44:40,552
" However , it ain 't funny , McGee . On the contrary , it 's pretty scary .

909
00:44:40,636 --> 00:44:45,557
" And Creature from the Black Lagoon is a first-rate thriller-chiller of its sci-fi type . "

910
00:44:45,850 --> 00:44:47,642
But when the movie used to play on New York TV ,

911
00:44:47,727 --> 00:44:50,854
the New York Times always called it " about as charming as a dead cat . "

912
00:44:52,899 --> 00:44:54,608
Believe it or not , there is such a thing as rotenone ,

913
00:44:54,692 --> 00:44:56,193
they didn 't make it up for this movie .

914
00:44:56,277 --> 00:44:59,362
It 's a crystalline substance used in insecticides .

915
00:44:59,447 --> 00:45:02,199
Universal decided not to use real rotenone in these scenes ,

916
00:45:02,283 --> 00:45:04,993
so they had to do some experimenting during preproduction .

917
00:45:05,077 --> 00:45:06,912
They combined pure cream with aluminum

918
00:45:06,996 --> 00:45:09,206
for shots of rotenone on the surface of the water ,

919
00:45:09,290 --> 00:45:11,833
and they used pure cream alone for the underwater shots .

920
00:45:18,549 --> 00:45:21,885
Here in one little rowboat are the two leading sci-fi stars of the ' 50s .

921
00:45:21,969 --> 00:45:24,221
Richard Carlson was in It Came from Outer Space ,

922
00:45:24,305 --> 00:45:27,390
The Magnetic Monster , The Maze and Riders to the Stars .

923
00:45:27,475 --> 00:45:30,310
And Richard Denning was in Target Earth , Creature with the Atom Brain ,

924
00:45:30,394 --> 00:45:32,604
The Day the World Ended , and Black Scorpion .

925
00:45:32,688 --> 00:45:34,564
And they were both in Black Lagoon , of course .

926
00:45:34,649 --> 00:45:36,691
So they 're even-steven , sci-fi-wise .

927
00:45:37,151 --> 00:45:38,902
When I talked to Denning and asked about Carlson ,

928
00:45:38,986 --> 00:45:40,487
he wasn 't crazy about the guy .

929
00:45:40,571 --> 00:45:42,697
Denning said Carlson could be uppity ,

930
00:45:42,782 --> 00:45:45,825
and that goes right along with what some other people have told me , too .

931
00:45:45,910 --> 00:45:48,119
The one who said it best was an actor named Michael Fox ,

932
00:45:48,204 --> 00:45:49,913
who worked with Carlson in Magnetic Monster

933
00:45:49,997 --> 00:45:53,041
and Riders to the Stars , and probably half a dozen other movies .

934
00:45:53,125 --> 00:45:56,586
He said Carlson envisioned himself an actor , writer , producer , director .

935
00:45:56,671 --> 00:45:59,839
And Carlson was all those things at various times , by the way .

936
00:45:59,924 --> 00:46:03,176
Fox said it was sad . Carlson was a good-looking guy , he was bright ,

937
00:46:03,261 --> 00:46:06,513
he was quite well read in many areas , but the moment Carlson thought

938
00:46:06,597 --> 00:46:10,100
that somebody else was in authority , he would tell that person how to do his job .

939
00:46:10,268 --> 00:46:14,437
According to Fox , " Carlson was used to being the intellectual limelight on a set .

940
00:46:14,522 --> 00:46:17,440
" He did not like to share it with someone who was , in his opinion ,

941
00:46:17,525 --> 00:46:19,067
" lesser than he . "

942
00:46:20,194 --> 00:46:23,655
And incidentally , Richard Denning , who 's out to bag the creature in this movie ,

943
00:46:23,739 --> 00:46:26,866
certainly did know his way around boats and nets and traps .

944
00:46:26,951 --> 00:46:29,786
When Denning got back from serving in the military during World War II ,

945
00:46:29,870 --> 00:46:33,623
he , like a lot of other young movie actors who 'd had their careers interrupted ,

946
00:46:33,791 --> 00:46:35,792
had a hell of a hard time getting it started again .

947
00:46:36,210 --> 00:46:39,296
He and his actress wife , Evelyn Ankers , had used up all their savings ,

948
00:46:39,380 --> 00:46:42,382
so what they did was move into a house trailer at Paradise Cove ,

949
00:46:42,550 --> 00:46:46,094
and he set up 100 lobster traps . Denning had always loved the water

950
00:46:46,178 --> 00:46:49,306
and boats and everything , so I guess this was almost second nature to him .

951
00:46:49,390 --> 00:46:51,558
He had a rowboat , he couldn 't afford an outboard ,

952
00:46:51,726 --> 00:46:53,143
and he would row the traps out ,

953
00:46:53,227 --> 00:46:56,021
set them in the evenings , and then go out and collect them at daybreak .

954
00:46:56,314 --> 00:46:58,815
The Dennings lived on lobster and sold lobster ,

955
00:46:58,899 --> 00:47:01,609
and years later , when he and Ankers looked back at that time ,

956
00:47:01,694 --> 00:47:03,653
they realized that those were their happiest times .

957
00:47:04,155 --> 00:47:05,655
But then Denning started getting work again .

958
00:47:05,740 --> 00:47:07,907
He played Lucille Ball 's husband on the radio show

959
00:47:07,992 --> 00:47:10,535
that was later changed for TV into I Love Lucy ,

960
00:47:10,619 --> 00:47:14,372
and he started starring in B-movies again and he let the lobster business go .

961
00:47:14,665 --> 00:47:18,835
He died in 1998 , and Richard Carlson died in 1977 .

962
00:47:23,549 --> 00:47:26,843
The Rita was a real boat out on Park Lake on the Universal back lot ,

963
00:47:26,927 --> 00:47:30,013
but some scenes on the Rita were shot indoors on a duplicate Rita ,

964
00:47:30,097 --> 00:47:31,890
on Universal 's process stage .

965
00:47:32,099 --> 00:47:35,477
The scenes at the beginning , for example , of Richard Denning firing the spear gun ,

966
00:47:35,561 --> 00:47:36,561
those were shot indoors

967
00:47:36,645 --> 00:47:39,731
with the jungle projected on a rear projection screen behind them .

968
00:47:39,815 --> 00:47:42,942
And this eerie nocturnal scene here was shot on the process stage ,

969
00:47:43,110 --> 00:47:44,652
at about 9 : 00 in the morning .

970
00:47:45,112 --> 00:47:46,905
The scenes in the creature 's cavernous grotto

971
00:47:46,989 --> 00:47:49,032
were also shot on a set on the process stage .

972
00:47:56,123 --> 00:47:58,416
Here 's Ben Chapman , about to climb onto the boat .

973
00:47:58,501 --> 00:48:00,877
Or , I should say , to try to climb onto the boat .

974
00:48:00,961 --> 00:48:03,671
Once The Gill Man suit got waterlogged , it weighed a ton ,

975
00:48:03,756 --> 00:48:06,758
and even someone as young and strong as Chapman couldn 't climb a rope

976
00:48:06,842 --> 00:48:09,552
with those slippery , wet gloves on and his suit weighing him down .

977
00:48:10,012 --> 00:48:13,181
So what they did was get a big stepladder and put it in the water ,

978
00:48:13,265 --> 00:48:15,308
the top step just below the surface .

979
00:48:15,518 --> 00:48:19,104
And Chapman 's walking up the ladder as he pretends to be climbing the rope .

980
00:48:19,814 --> 00:48:22,357
But the mistake they made was , when he gets scared by the lantern

981
00:48:22,441 --> 00:48:24,484
and lets go of the rope , for a split second ,

982
00:48:24,568 --> 00:48:26,569
you can tell that he 's standing on something in the water .

983
00:48:26,654 --> 00:48:28,029
When Chapman let go of the rope ,

984
00:48:28,114 --> 00:48:30,740
he should 've just dropped straight down into the water like a rock ,

985
00:48:30,825 --> 00:48:33,284
but instead , he stood on the surface of the water for a split second

986
00:48:33,369 --> 00:48:34,744
and then fell off to the side .

987
00:48:35,913 --> 00:48:38,498
Creature from the Black Lagoon was Jack Arnold 's fourth feature film

988
00:48:38,582 --> 00:48:39,707
and his third in 3-D .

989
00:48:39,917 --> 00:48:41,835
He 'd already directed It Came from Outer Space

990
00:48:41,919 --> 00:48:44,838
and a murder drama called The Glass Web with Edward G. Robinson .

991
00:48:45,172 --> 00:48:48,091
Jack Arnold was born on a kitchen table in New Haven , Connecticut ,

992
00:48:48,175 --> 00:48:49,884
the son of young Russian immigrants .

993
00:48:49,969 --> 00:48:53,346
He was raised in New York and began acting on Broadway in the 1930s .

994
00:48:53,431 --> 00:48:55,098
During the war , he worked as a cameraman

995
00:48:55,182 --> 00:48:57,767
for documentary filmmaker Robert Flaherty 's unit .

996
00:48:57,852 --> 00:49:00,812
After his discharge , he made educational and promotional films

997
00:49:00,896 --> 00:49:04,315
with titles like Chicken of Tomorrow for the Agriculture Department ,

998
00:49:04,400 --> 00:49:07,193
while at the same time , continuing to act on stage .

999
00:49:07,278 --> 00:49:09,779
In 1950 , he directed a public-relations film

1000
00:49:09,864 --> 00:49:13,450
for the International Ladies ' Garment Workers ' Union , With These Hands .

1001
00:49:13,701 --> 00:49:14,951
The film starred Broadway actors

1002
00:49:15,035 --> 00:49:17,704
Sam Levene , Arlene Francis and Joseph Wiseman .

1003
00:49:18,038 --> 00:49:22,625
With These Hands went into general theatrical release , became a critical success ,

1004
00:49:22,710 --> 00:49:26,212
and received an Academy Award nomination as best documentary feature .

1005
00:49:26,297 --> 00:49:28,923
Off of that project , he was discovered by Universal ,

1006
00:49:29,008 --> 00:49:31,509
who placed him under contract in 1952 .

1007
00:49:31,594 --> 00:49:35,180
In addition to the movies he directed , he also produced and directed for TV ,

1008
00:49:35,264 --> 00:49:38,516
including such series as It Takes a Thief and Gilligan 's Island .

1009
00:49:38,601 --> 00:49:42,562
He was one of the first major sci-fi directors , and as a result ,

1010
00:49:42,646 --> 00:49:45,523
his work was , in later years , honored at film festivals ,

1011
00:49:45,608 --> 00:49:49,110
in Mill Valley , California , France , Spain and Brazil .

1012
00:49:49,195 --> 00:49:51,905
In 1982 , Creature from the Black Lagoon was going to be remade

1013
00:49:51,989 --> 00:49:55,867
in color and 3D , a big budget , and with Jack Arnold at the helm .

1014
00:49:56,160 --> 00:49:58,661
John Landis was the one who brought the project to Arnold .

1015
00:49:58,746 --> 00:50:00,622
Landis was going to executive produce ,

1016
00:50:00,706 --> 00:50:02,874
Rick Baker was chosen to furnish the creature ,

1017
00:50:02,958 --> 00:50:05,418
based on the same classic design and makeup effects ,

1018
00:50:05,503 --> 00:50:09,005
and Nigel Kneale wrote the screenplay , but this new Creature was never made .

1019
00:50:09,089 --> 00:50:12,592
Universal decided to put their money behind Jaws 3-D instead .

1020
00:50:12,760 --> 00:50:14,469
Jack Arnold died in 1992 .

1021
00:50:15,930 --> 00:50:17,764
I never quite understood how they managed to dive

1022
00:50:17,848 --> 00:50:20,308
down , down , down , down , to the creature 's lair

1023
00:50:20,392 --> 00:50:23,228
and wind up in a cave that 's at sea level , with a beach entrance .

1024
00:50:23,896 --> 00:50:26,356
You 'd also think it would be as dark as the ace of spades in there .

1025
00:50:35,950 --> 00:50:38,660
This hand-on-the-shoulder gimmick is such a cliché today ,

1026
00:50:38,744 --> 00:50:41,454
but it still worked in 1954 . It got a great reaction .

1027
00:50:41,789 --> 00:50:44,874
Ernest Nims remembered a scene in It Came from Outer Space

1028
00:50:44,959 --> 00:50:47,085
where Barbara Rush did the same thing to Carlson ,

1029
00:50:47,169 --> 00:50:48,836
came up from behind and touched him .

1030
00:50:48,921 --> 00:50:51,965
It worked there , so Nims suggested they do it again in Creature .

1031
00:50:52,049 --> 00:50:53,550
And again it got a scream .

1032
00:50:53,634 --> 00:50:54,926
People who saw Creature at a preview

1033
00:50:55,010 --> 00:50:57,345
mentioned it on their preview comment cards .

1034
00:50:57,429 --> 00:51:00,014
Well , when something worked , Universal never let go .

1035
00:51:00,224 --> 00:51:03,268
They used it again in Tarantula , with John Agar on the receiving end ,

1036
00:51:03,352 --> 00:51:06,896
The Deadly Mantis with Craig Stevens , Cult of the Cobra with David Janssen ,

1037
00:51:06,981 --> 00:51:08,773
Monster on the Campus with Joanna Moore ,

1038
00:51:08,899 --> 00:51:10,817
right down the line for the rest of the decade .

1039
00:51:14,446 --> 00:51:15,613
One of the creature 's favorite habits

1040
00:51:15,698 --> 00:51:18,032
was to pick guys up over his head and throw them .

1041
00:51:18,158 --> 00:51:21,077
He did it to the college student on the beach in Revenge of the Creature ,

1042
00:51:21,161 --> 00:51:24,205
and he did it to Jeff Morrow at the end of Creature Walks Among Us .

1043
00:51:24,290 --> 00:51:27,166
And the plan here was for the creature to pick Zee up and throw him .

1044
00:51:27,251 --> 00:51:31,212
According to the script , Zee was going to be thrown into the camera for the 3-D effect .

1045
00:51:31,505 --> 00:51:33,464
The actor , Bernie Gozier , was on a wire ,

1046
00:51:33,549 --> 00:51:35,883
and he was going to be pulled up into the air that way ,

1047
00:51:35,968 --> 00:51:38,303
once Ben Chapman grabbed him and pretended to lift him .

1048
00:51:38,846 --> 00:51:40,972
Well , they tried it twice and twice the wire broke .

1049
00:51:41,098 --> 00:51:42,849
And finally , Jack Arnold decided to forget it ,

1050
00:51:42,933 --> 00:51:45,435
have the creature just grab and choke the guy instead .

1051
00:51:45,519 --> 00:51:48,896
But if you watch close enough , you can see in this shot coming up here

1052
00:51:48,981 --> 00:51:51,357
that the creature does reach down for the guy 's leg .

1053
00:51:52,192 --> 00:51:54,861
They shot that before they found out the wire gag wasn 't going to work .

1054
00:51:56,697 --> 00:51:58,781
Julie Adams says Creature was a very pleasant movie

1055
00:51:58,866 --> 00:52:00,283
and that they all laughed a lot .

1056
00:52:00,367 --> 00:52:02,493
And she particularly liked this scene here .

1057
00:52:02,578 --> 00:52:04,829
" We were down on the beach on the edge of the water one day

1058
00:52:04,913 --> 00:52:07,373
" doing the scene where the creature has killed one of the natives .

1059
00:52:07,458 --> 00:52:08,791
" We were looking down at the body

1060
00:52:08,876 --> 00:52:11,461
" and there was this moment where we were all very still .

1061
00:52:11,545 --> 00:52:15,298
" Richard Carlson was in his bathing trunks and he had his facemask on his forehead ,

1062
00:52:15,382 --> 00:52:18,301
" and in just the way people take off their hats for the dead ,

1063
00:52:18,385 --> 00:52:20,428
" he reached up and he took off his facemask .

1064
00:52:20,554 --> 00:52:23,264
" He doffed it so seriously that we all started to laugh ,

1065
00:52:23,349 --> 00:52:27,226
" and it took a long time to come back together again . It was just too absurd .

1066
00:52:27,353 --> 00:52:30,313
" And then we had this wonderful guy who did the clapboard for the different shots ,

1067
00:52:30,397 --> 00:52:31,981
" an old vaudevillian .

1068
00:52:32,066 --> 00:52:34,817
" The day Whit Bissell 's face was all bloodied up by the creature ,

1069
00:52:34,902 --> 00:52:37,278
" this fellow clapped the board , and then he said to Whit ... "

1070
00:52:37,446 --> 00:52:39,364
Julie said this out of the corner of her mouth ,

1071
00:52:39,573 --> 00:52:40,698
" How are you fixed for blades ? "

1072
00:52:42,409 --> 00:52:44,160
Jack Arnold had the same attitude about The Gill Man

1073
00:52:44,244 --> 00:52:45,912
that screenwriter Arthur Ross did .

1074
00:52:45,996 --> 00:52:49,582
Arnold said in interviews that he set out to make it a very sympathetic character .

1075
00:52:49,958 --> 00:52:52,752
In one interview , Arnold said the creature was violent

1076
00:52:52,836 --> 00:52:55,963
" because he 's provoked into violence . Inherent in the character

1077
00:52:56,048 --> 00:52:58,424
" is the statement that all of us have violence within ,

1078
00:52:58,509 --> 00:53:01,886
" and if provoked , are capable of any bizarre retaliation .

1079
00:53:01,970 --> 00:53:03,429
" If left alone and understood ,

1080
00:53:03,514 --> 00:53:07,308
" that 's when we overcome the primeval urges that we are all cursed with .

1081
00:53:07,393 --> 00:53:08,893
" Man 's inhumanity to man

1082
00:53:08,977 --> 00:53:11,646
" means not only man 's inhumanity to his own kind ,

1083
00:53:11,730 --> 00:53:15,650
" but to anything else , especially something that 's very different from himself .

1084
00:53:15,734 --> 00:53:19,362
" You can trace the roots down to primitive tribes , one against the other ,

1085
00:53:19,446 --> 00:53:23,324
" in the cities , to this block against the next block , or the Jew against the Arab ,

1086
00:53:23,409 --> 00:53:26,536
" the Protestant against the Catholic , the black against the white .

1087
00:53:26,620 --> 00:53:28,162
" We have not progressed as human beings

1088
00:53:28,247 --> 00:53:31,374
" to differentiate between what is superficial and what is not .

1089
00:53:31,458 --> 00:53:34,544
" Of course , the sooner we learn the lesson , the better off we 'll be .

1090
00:53:34,628 --> 00:53:37,130
" And that 's what I tried to point out in my science fiction films ,

1091
00:53:37,214 --> 00:53:39,465
" in a manner in which an audience would accept it .

1092
00:53:39,550 --> 00:53:42,593
" I don 't think they would accept a polemic . They 'd walk out on it ,

1093
00:53:42,678 --> 00:53:45,805
" or it 'd be under investigation by the House Un-American Activities Committee ,

1094
00:53:45,973 --> 00:53:47,223
" or some such animal .

1095
00:53:47,307 --> 00:53:51,477
" My objective was primarily to entertain , but I also wanted to say something .

1096
00:53:51,562 --> 00:53:54,897
" If 10 % of the audience grasped it , then I was very successful . "

1097
00:54:04,408 --> 00:54:06,534
Whit Bissell , who 's in a number of Jack Arnold movies ,

1098
00:54:06,618 --> 00:54:08,369
was a versatile character actor ,

1099
00:54:08,454 --> 00:54:10,997
and an ideal choice for the part of the gentle Dr. Thompson .

1100
00:54:11,290 --> 00:54:14,417
But he was just as ideally suited for his ruthless mad-scientist roles

1101
00:54:14,501 --> 00:54:17,837
in movies like I Was a Teenage Werewolf and I Was a Teenage Frankenstein .

1102
00:54:18,338 --> 00:54:21,632
He was one of the most recognizable of 1950s supporting players .

1103
00:54:21,842 --> 00:54:24,010
He 's also in sci-fi pictures like Lost Continent ,

1104
00:54:24,094 --> 00:54:25,636
Target Earth with Richard Denning ,

1105
00:54:25,721 --> 00:54:28,931
Invasion of the Body Snatchers , Jack Arnold 's Monster on the Campus ,

1106
00:54:29,016 --> 00:54:31,976
The Time Machine , The Manchurian Candidate , Soylent Green ,

1107
00:54:32,060 --> 00:54:34,103
and Psychic Killer with Julie Adams .

1108
00:54:34,188 --> 00:54:37,398
Bissell was a native of New York City . He worked on the stage from boyhood ,

1109
00:54:37,483 --> 00:54:41,944
and appeared in a number of Broadway plays before he went Hollywood in the mid-1940s .

1110
00:54:42,029 --> 00:54:44,781
He worked in movies at first under the name Whitner Bissell ,

1111
00:54:44,865 --> 00:54:48,868
and he was in pictures like Holy Matrimony , Destination Tokyo and Brute Force .

1112
00:54:49,036 --> 00:54:51,454
His busiest decade for film work was the ' 50s .

1113
00:54:51,538 --> 00:54:53,748
He made more than 50 movies in 10 years .

1114
00:54:53,832 --> 00:54:56,209
A lot of small parts , but a few co-starring roles , too .

1115
00:54:56,668 --> 00:54:58,503
He was also in lots of sci-fi TV series ,

1116
00:54:58,587 --> 00:55:01,380
and he was a regular on Irwin Allen 's Land of the Giants .

1117
00:55:01,465 --> 00:55:03,508
Whit Bissell died in 1996 .

1118
00:55:05,552 --> 00:55:06,636
( DOOR CLATTERING )

1119
00:55:06,720 --> 00:55:09,096
It 's a little hard to believe that Bissell , or anybody else ,

1120
00:55:09,181 --> 00:55:12,600
could fall asleep guarding a seven-foot-tall killer from the dawn of time .

1121
00:55:12,684 --> 00:55:14,268
Maybe it 's that soothing music ,

1122
00:55:14,353 --> 00:55:17,063
a Henry Mancini composition called Monster Caught .

1123
00:55:17,856 --> 00:55:19,315
It 's funny to think of Henry Mancini

1124
00:55:19,399 --> 00:55:21,609
writing music for these Universal monster movies ,

1125
00:55:21,693 --> 00:55:25,363
and without credit at that , but this obviously was years before Peter Gunn

1126
00:55:25,447 --> 00:55:27,240
and his several Academy Awards

1127
00:55:27,324 --> 00:55:31,035
for Breakfast At Tiffany 's and Moon River and Days of Wine and Roses .

1128
00:55:31,119 --> 00:55:35,081
He also won over 20 GRAMMYs . And in 1990 , David Schecter tells me ,

1129
00:55:35,165 --> 00:55:38,167
Mancini put out a CD called Mancini in Surround ,

1130
00:55:38,252 --> 00:55:40,628
and on it , he included some music he wrote for Creature ,

1131
00:55:40,754 --> 00:55:43,089
an excellent piece called Monster Gets Mark .

1132
00:55:43,173 --> 00:55:46,926
You 'll hear it later during the scene where the creature fights and kills Richard Denning .

1133
00:55:47,010 --> 00:55:49,971
Mancini re-orchestrated and slightly recomposed the cues ,

1134
00:55:50,055 --> 00:55:52,640
and he conducted them at a much more leisurely pace ,

1135
00:55:52,766 --> 00:55:55,017
but it was still clearly Creature music .

1136
00:55:55,102 --> 00:55:57,854
Mancini also contributed to the scores of It Came from Outer Space

1137
00:55:57,938 --> 00:56:00,189
and Tarantula , and lots of other golden oldies .

1138
00:56:00,274 --> 00:56:04,318
KAY : If it weren 't for Mark Williams , I wouldn 't have my work or even a job .

1139
00:56:04,403 --> 00:56:07,738
DR. THOMPSON : It 's true , he helped you through your training and gave you a job .

1140
00:56:07,823 --> 00:56:10,408
But he needed you just as much as you needed him .

1141
00:56:10,701 --> 00:56:11,993
You 're oversimplifying .

1142
00:56:12,077 --> 00:56:14,954
You 've more than repaid him many times over .

1143
00:56:15,038 --> 00:56:17,164
Why , a good part of his present position at the institute

1144
00:56:17,249 --> 00:56:19,041
is due to your valuable research .

1145
00:56:19,126 --> 00:56:20,126
And another thing ...

1146
00:56:20,252 --> 00:56:21,627
WEAVER : Whit Bissell is going to strike the creature

1147
00:56:21,712 --> 00:56:23,754
with a lantern in a minute , setting him afire .

1148
00:56:23,839 --> 00:56:27,925
How they did this was they got a stunt man named Al Wyatt , Rock Hudson 's stunt man ,

1149
00:56:28,010 --> 00:56:30,386
and they put him in a fire suit and they set him on fire .

1150
00:56:30,470 --> 00:56:33,514
Then they took that footage and they superimposed it over this shot here

1151
00:56:33,599 --> 00:56:37,518
of Ben Chapman , of the creature , waving his arms and pretending to be on fire .

1152
00:56:38,645 --> 00:56:41,063
But Bob Burns tells me that in 3D , the scene didn 't work .

1153
00:56:41,148 --> 00:56:44,942
He says that Wyatt must 've been closer to the 3D camera when he did his part

1154
00:56:45,027 --> 00:56:47,194
than Chapman was when Chapman did his scene .

1155
00:56:47,404 --> 00:56:49,280
Burns says that in 3-D , the creature and the fire

1156
00:56:49,364 --> 00:56:51,073
seem to be different distances away .

1157
00:56:51,158 --> 00:56:52,325
( GROANING )

1158
00:57:00,918 --> 00:57:02,168
WEAVER : Did you see that ? Did you notice the way

1159
00:57:02,252 --> 00:57:03,502
Antonio Moreno gave the impression

1160
00:57:03,587 --> 00:57:06,631
he was about to cover Whit Bissell with a sheet , like Bissell was dead ,

1161
00:57:06,715 --> 00:57:08,341
and then he put the sheet down again ?

1162
00:57:08,926 --> 00:57:12,637
I don 't know if they did that on purpose or not , but either way , it was a great fake-out .

1163
00:57:12,804 --> 00:57:14,764
Tough as Ricou Browning and Ben Chapman had it ,

1164
00:57:14,848 --> 00:57:16,557
the stunt man who played The Gill Man in the sequel ,

1165
00:57:16,642 --> 00:57:19,393
Revenge of the Creature , probably had it even tougher .

1166
00:57:19,478 --> 00:57:23,230
The Gill Man suits for Ricou and Ben were originally made in multiple pieces ,

1167
00:57:23,315 --> 00:57:27,026
and they had what are called flex gaps near the elbows , the shoulders and knees ,

1168
00:57:27,194 --> 00:57:30,780
which gave Ricou and Ben a little bit of freedom and mobility whenever they moved .

1169
00:57:31,114 --> 00:57:34,617
But The Gill Man suit worn by stunt man Tom Hennesy in Revenge of the Creature

1170
00:57:34,701 --> 00:57:36,535
was a molded one-piece bodysuit ,

1171
00:57:36,703 --> 00:57:39,956
which Bob Burns , who 's had to wear suits like that in his time ,

1172
00:57:40,040 --> 00:57:41,290
tells me are not made to flex ,

1173
00:57:41,375 --> 00:57:44,251
they give the guy inside no freedom , and they 're very uncomfortable .

1174
00:57:44,711 --> 00:57:46,629
There were two sequels to Creature from the Black Lagoon .

1175
00:57:46,713 --> 00:57:51,634
Revenge of the Creature , made in 1954 , and The Creature Walks Among Us , made in 1955 .

1176
00:57:51,718 --> 00:57:54,512
Ricou Browning played the underwater Gill Man in all three .

1177
00:57:54,596 --> 00:57:57,473
In Revenge , the above-water Gill Man was played by Tom Hennesy ,

1178
00:57:57,557 --> 00:58:00,935
and in Creature Walks , where the creature is seriously burned in a fire ,

1179
00:58:01,019 --> 00:58:03,938
the smooth-skinned Gill Man was played by actor Don Megowan .

1180
00:58:04,982 --> 00:58:07,984
The only name you 'll see listed in the credits for makeup is Bud Westmore ,

1181
00:58:08,068 --> 00:58:09,902
but he was just the head of the makeup department .

1182
00:58:09,987 --> 00:58:11,570
He did very little of the work .

1183
00:58:11,655 --> 00:58:15,116
He liked to make out he did , he was a self-promoter and a publicity hound ,

1184
00:58:15,200 --> 00:58:17,576
and for most every Universal international monster movie ,

1185
00:58:17,661 --> 00:58:21,163
there are a bunch of photos of Westmore painstakingly working on the thing ,

1186
00:58:21,248 --> 00:58:23,874
Westmore in his white shirt and tie and his sweater .

1187
00:58:23,959 --> 00:58:26,961
I mean , that was a tip-off right there . Nobody who 's really doing any work

1188
00:58:27,045 --> 00:58:29,880
in a makeup lab wears an outfit like that while he 's working .

1189
00:58:29,965 --> 00:58:32,842
But that 's how Westmore would show up when photos were going to be taken .

1190
00:58:33,510 --> 00:58:37,346
There 's a funny story I shouldn 't tell , but I will , about sculptor Chris Mueller ,

1191
00:58:37,431 --> 00:58:40,683
the very talented guy who sculpted The Gill Man 's head and hands .

1192
00:58:40,767 --> 00:58:44,395
He started out as an architectural sculptor , and his career went back aways .

1193
00:58:44,479 --> 00:58:48,649
He worked on Sabu , Thief of Bagdad , sculpting the temple and the all-seeing god .

1194
00:58:48,734 --> 00:58:50,192
He sculpted animals for The Jungle Book ,

1195
00:58:50,277 --> 00:58:53,404
and the giant squid for the Disney 20000 Leagues Under the Sea ,

1196
00:58:53,488 --> 00:58:56,532
and he also worked at Universal , doing architectural stuff at first ,

1197
00:58:56,616 --> 00:58:58,868
and then gravitating into the makeup department .

1198
00:58:58,952 --> 00:59:02,163
He sculpted the hunchback and the phantom for Man of a Thousand Faces ,

1199
00:59:02,247 --> 00:59:04,582
the Hyde mask for A and C Meet Jekyll and Hyde ,

1200
00:59:04,666 --> 00:59:06,959
the mummy for A and C Meet the Mummy , et cetera , et cetera .

1201
00:59:07,127 --> 00:59:10,713
Anyway , one day Mueller is there at Universal , working on The Gill Man head ,

1202
00:59:10,797 --> 00:59:12,173
and somebody else there tipped him off .

1203
00:59:12,257 --> 00:59:14,884
The guy told Mueller , " Look , if Bud Westmore comes in here

1204
00:59:14,968 --> 00:59:17,762
" and tells you to take the rest of the day off , don 't do it .

1205
00:59:17,888 --> 00:59:20,765
" What that means is that the press is gonna be coming in to take pictures ,

1206
00:59:20,849 --> 00:59:22,725
" and Westmore doesn 't want you around . "

1207
00:59:23,060 --> 00:59:25,394
Well , Mueller goes back to work and some time goes by ,

1208
00:59:25,479 --> 00:59:27,438
and sure enough , in comes Bud Westmore .

1209
00:59:27,522 --> 00:59:30,524
Westmore looks at the creature head Mueller 's sculpting and he says ,

1210
00:59:30,609 --> 00:59:33,944
" Oh , Chris , that 's just great . Man , you 're doin ' a terrific job .

1211
00:59:34,029 --> 00:59:36,363
" Hey , look , why don 't you take the rest of the day off ? "

1212
00:59:36,448 --> 00:59:39,950
And Mueller , still working on the creature head , tells Westmore , " No , I wanna finish . "

1213
00:59:40,452 --> 00:59:42,661
Again Westmore tells him , " Chris , look , you 're doing great work ,

1214
00:59:42,746 --> 00:59:44,538
" take some time off , take it easy . "

1215
00:59:44,623 --> 00:59:47,625
But Mueller doesn 't budge . " No , no , no . I don 't wanna go . I 'm almost finished .

1216
00:59:47,709 --> 00:59:50,336
" I 'm so close to being finished . I can finish this today . "

1217
00:59:51,088 --> 00:59:52,755
And what could Westmore say to that ?

1218
00:59:52,881 --> 00:59:56,967
Well , out Westmore goes and then half an hour , or an hour , or whatever later ,

1219
00:59:57,052 --> 01:00:01,180
sure enough , here comes Westmore back in again , this time with the press corps .

1220
01:00:01,264 --> 01:00:04,266
Westmore in his sporty little outfit , goes over to where Mueller is working ,

1221
01:00:04,351 --> 01:00:06,102
and he picks up one of Mueller 's sculpting tools

1222
01:00:06,186 --> 01:00:08,521
and he holds the thing right up to the creature head ,

1223
01:00:08,605 --> 01:00:10,022
but he doesn 't even touch the head with the tool

1224
01:00:10,107 --> 01:00:12,608
probably because he didn 't know what the hell he was doing .

1225
01:00:12,692 --> 01:00:15,402
Mueller 's standing behind the creature head , still working ,

1226
01:00:15,487 --> 01:00:17,238
and the photographer starts snapping away .

1227
01:00:18,031 --> 01:00:20,449
Anyway , the pictures are published and the captions say ,

1228
01:00:20,534 --> 01:00:23,202
" Bud Westmore at work on his newest creation , the creature . "

1229
01:00:23,453 --> 01:00:26,622
No mention of Mueller , but sure enough , there he was in the pictures .

1230
01:00:26,832 --> 01:00:29,250
Because of where Mueller was standing , they couldn 't crop him out .

1231
01:00:29,668 --> 01:00:32,419
Mueller got a kick out of that , and he used to enjoy telling that story .

1232
01:00:32,796 --> 01:00:35,172
- Lucas . Lucas , start your winch . - Eh ?

1233
01:00:36,133 --> 01:00:38,717
WEAVER : The Gill Man outfit was very expensive , needless to say .

1234
01:00:38,885 --> 01:00:40,845
According to Universal 's publicity department ,

1235
01:00:40,929 --> 01:00:43,139
which probably inflated some of these figures ,

1236
01:00:43,223 --> 01:00:45,307
it was valued at $ 18,000 ,

1237
01:00:45,392 --> 01:00:48,769
and was the product of eight-and-a-half months of research and experimentation .

1238
01:00:48,895 --> 01:00:51,313
" Fashioned of foam rubber , plastic ,

1239
01:00:51,398 --> 01:00:53,941
" hidden controls and specially-tailored fittings ,

1240
01:00:54,025 --> 01:00:57,653
" the costume forms the end result of 76 different body sketches ,

1241
01:00:57,737 --> 01:00:59,405
" 32 different head models ,

1242
01:00:59,489 --> 01:01:04,493
" and 176 pounds of foam rubber used during the experimental stages . "

1243
01:01:04,578 --> 01:01:07,163
There were articles about The Gill Man suit in different magazines ,

1244
01:01:07,247 --> 01:01:10,499
including non-movie magazines like Mechanix Illustrated .

1245
01:01:10,584 --> 01:01:14,461
There were also behind-the-scenes photos and an article in Skin Diver magazine .

1246
01:01:14,546 --> 01:01:17,715
According to Skin Diver , the August 1954 issue ,

1247
01:01:17,799 --> 01:01:20,176
Creature , which had only been out a couple of months at that point ,

1248
01:01:20,260 --> 01:01:21,594
had already grossed two million .

1249
01:01:21,678 --> 01:01:23,512
- Take it away ! - ( WINCH WHIRRING )

1250
01:01:23,597 --> 01:01:24,555
Take it up .

1251
01:01:24,639 --> 01:01:26,015
More , more .

1252
01:01:27,225 --> 01:01:29,727
WEAVER : Hey , the cracked boom has mysteriously fixed itself .

1253
01:01:30,437 --> 01:01:32,813
I mentioned earlier that The Gill Man outfit had flex gaps

1254
01:01:32,898 --> 01:01:35,983
that gave the people inside a little bit of freedom , but not a heck of a lot ,

1255
01:01:36,067 --> 01:01:37,860
and obviously , it was a lot of time and trouble

1256
01:01:37,944 --> 01:01:39,945
getting the actors in and out of the costume .

1257
01:01:40,030 --> 01:01:41,197
When it was time for lunch ,

1258
01:01:41,281 --> 01:01:43,991
Ben Chapman would take off the helmet , the boots and the gloves ,

1259
01:01:44,075 --> 01:01:46,410
and they had for him a stand-up chair .

1260
01:01:46,494 --> 01:01:50,539
It looked like a chaise lounge , except that it stood straight up and had armrests on it .

1261
01:01:50,624 --> 01:01:54,335
He 'd get into that , and there would be a very high table for him to eat off of .

1262
01:01:55,128 --> 01:01:57,922
Ben Chapman also played the creature on TV in 1954 .

1263
01:01:58,006 --> 01:02:01,675
He had a gag-cameo appearance in an episode of The Colgate Comedy Hour ,

1264
01:02:01,760 --> 01:02:05,971
with Abbott and Costello , and , playing the Frankenstein monster , Glenn Strange .

1265
01:02:06,056 --> 01:02:08,057
Universal , no doubt , considered TV a threat ,

1266
01:02:08,141 --> 01:02:10,392
the way most every other studio in Hollywood did ,

1267
01:02:10,477 --> 01:02:12,645
but they used it to promote the movie anyway .

1268
01:02:12,729 --> 01:02:15,397
They let Chapman go on The Colgate Comedy Hour .

1269
01:02:15,482 --> 01:02:17,650
And Julie Adams made some promotional appearances ,

1270
01:02:17,734 --> 01:02:19,902
and they even had a trailer for the movie running on TV ,

1271
01:02:19,986 --> 01:02:21,904
which caused a little bit of a squawk .

1272
01:02:21,988 --> 01:02:24,406
A couple of TV stations refused to run it ,

1273
01:02:24,491 --> 01:02:27,618
but at least one person at Universal took that as a compliment to the movie ,

1274
01:02:27,702 --> 01:02:28,827
and he wrote in a memo ,

1275
01:02:28,912 --> 01:02:31,622
" I can 't think of higher praise for what we have to sell on TV

1276
01:02:31,706 --> 01:02:33,290
" for Creature from the Black Lagoon ,

1277
01:02:33,375 --> 01:02:35,000
" than to have a station refuse a spot

1278
01:02:35,085 --> 01:02:37,586
" because it considered the material too scary .

1279
01:02:37,671 --> 01:02:42,549
" We have had two such incidents locally , one with KTTV , which is a DuMont affiliate ,

1280
01:02:42,634 --> 01:02:47,429
" and KNBH , the NBC station . In both instances , it related primarily

1281
01:02:47,514 --> 01:02:50,683
" to placement between programs with large juvenile followings .

1282
01:02:50,850 --> 01:02:55,354
" We finally won our point with KNBH , but it took considerable haranguing . "

1283
01:02:59,192 --> 01:03:01,986
Creature had the same producer , and director , and some of the same writers ,

1284
01:03:02,112 --> 01:03:05,281
as It Came from Outer Space , so it isn 't surprising that a few echoes

1285
01:03:05,365 --> 01:03:07,241
from that plot should turn up in this movie .

1286
01:03:07,325 --> 01:03:08,409
In It Came from Outer Space ,

1287
01:03:08,493 --> 01:03:10,536
Richard Carlson was an egghead and a peacenik

1288
01:03:10,620 --> 01:03:12,454
who wants the Xenomorphs left alone ,

1289
01:03:12,539 --> 01:03:14,873
and throughout the movie , he clashes with the hard-headed sheriff ,

1290
01:03:14,958 --> 01:03:16,792
and it culminated in a fistfight .

1291
01:03:16,876 --> 01:03:19,586
The relationship between Carlson and Richard Denning in Creature

1292
01:03:19,671 --> 01:03:22,631
develops pretty much the same way , and they too come to blows ,

1293
01:03:22,716 --> 01:03:24,842
at almost precisely the same spot in the picture .

1294
01:03:25,802 --> 01:03:28,304
The New York Times , in their original review of Creature , complained ,

1295
01:03:28,388 --> 01:03:30,264
" It 's a fishing expedition that is necessary

1296
01:03:30,348 --> 01:03:32,891
" only if a viewer has lost all of his comic books .

1297
01:03:32,976 --> 01:03:35,686
" The proceedings above and underwater were filmed in 3-D

1298
01:03:35,770 --> 01:03:39,148
" to impart an illusion of depth when viewed through polarized glasses .

1299
01:03:39,232 --> 01:03:41,150
" This adventure has no depth . "

1300
01:03:41,234 --> 01:03:45,195
Variety said , " The below-water scraps between skin-divers and the prehistoric thing

1301
01:03:45,280 --> 01:03:48,365
" are thrilling and will pop goose pimples on the susceptible fan ,

1302
01:03:48,450 --> 01:03:51,160
" as will the close-up scenes of the scaly , gilled creature .

1303
01:03:51,286 --> 01:03:56,665
" Jack Arnold 's direction has done a first-rate job of developing chills and suspense . "

1304
01:03:56,750 --> 01:04:00,294
The Hollywood Reporter : " Jack Arnold 's megging is briskly competent ,

1305
01:04:00,378 --> 01:04:03,589
" although too much time is wasted on underwater shots which are neither novel

1306
01:04:03,673 --> 01:04:06,467
" or dramatic enough to hold interest for the entire footage . "

1307
01:04:07,677 --> 01:04:09,178
We 're about to go underwater again .

1308
01:04:09,262 --> 01:04:12,056
This movie has about 18 minutes of underwater footage .

1309
01:04:12,140 --> 01:04:14,266
Everybody working underwater had scuba gear ,

1310
01:04:14,434 --> 01:04:17,227
but the way Ricou Browning worked underwater as The Gill Man was ,

1311
01:04:17,312 --> 01:04:19,146
he did what is called " hose-breathing . "

1312
01:04:19,230 --> 01:04:21,607
That 's something he knew how to do long before Creature .

1313
01:04:21,733 --> 01:04:22,858
Ricou says that his old boss ,

1314
01:04:22,942 --> 01:04:25,694
Newton Perry , the manager of Wakulla Springs ,

1315
01:04:25,779 --> 01:04:29,365
was probably one of the first people to ever breathe from an air hose underwater ,

1316
01:04:29,449 --> 01:04:31,992
and he taught Ricou and other people how to do it .

1317
01:04:32,077 --> 01:04:34,203
You 'd have an oxygen tank at the bottom of the spring

1318
01:04:34,287 --> 01:04:37,039
or , coming from the surface , just an air hose .

1319
01:04:37,123 --> 01:04:39,833
Ricou could stick the air hose in his mouth and breathe from it ,

1320
01:04:39,918 --> 01:04:42,252
like you would drink water from a hose in your back yard .

1321
01:04:42,629 --> 01:04:44,505
Ricou says they used to do hose-breathing

1322
01:04:44,589 --> 01:04:46,924
in the underwater shows at Weeki Wachee Springs .

1323
01:04:47,008 --> 01:04:49,134
That was how the mermaids in the show got their air .

1324
01:04:49,219 --> 01:04:51,470
Anyway , it was something Ricou could do very naturally .

1325
01:04:51,554 --> 01:04:53,889
He could just insert the hose in the mouth of the creature ,

1326
01:04:53,973 --> 01:04:58,227
then insert it a couple more inches to get it to his own mouth , and then breathe .

1327
01:04:58,311 --> 01:05:00,104
Ricou had several helpers and safety men

1328
01:05:00,188 --> 01:05:02,356
working with him at the bottom of Wakulla Springs ,

1329
01:05:02,482 --> 01:05:05,984
guys he 'd brought in himself . Some of them had worked with him in water shows ,

1330
01:05:06,069 --> 01:05:08,070
others had done some cave-diving with him .

1331
01:05:08,363 --> 01:05:12,324
As he was getting ready to do a scene , Ricou would just be down there underwater ,

1332
01:05:12,409 --> 01:05:16,036
ready to go , breathing from an air hose , with a safety man nearby .

1333
01:05:16,121 --> 01:05:19,665
When he was ready to go into a scene , he 'd give the cameraman a hand signal ,

1334
01:05:19,749 --> 01:05:21,792
but he would just stay in place , still breathing ,

1335
01:05:21,876 --> 01:05:25,337
waiting for the cameraman to signal him that the camera was rolling .

1336
01:05:25,422 --> 01:05:27,756
Ricou would then hand the hose to his safety man ,

1337
01:05:27,841 --> 01:05:29,800
swim into the scene and do what he had to do .

1338
01:05:29,884 --> 01:05:33,387
Let 's say it was just a swim-through , so he 'd swim right past the cameraman .

1339
01:05:33,471 --> 01:05:37,099
Then on the other side , there 'd be another safety man with another air hose .

1340
01:05:37,183 --> 01:05:40,102
Between the fact that he could hold his breath for a long period of time ,

1341
01:05:40,186 --> 01:05:42,438
and all the air hoses and safety men down there ,

1342
01:05:42,522 --> 01:05:45,732
he was able to stay underwater in the spring for long periods of time ,

1343
01:05:45,817 --> 01:05:49,027
even though he was the only person down there with no scuba gear .

1344
01:05:49,112 --> 01:05:52,197
If he got in trouble , if he ran out of air with no one nearby ,

1345
01:05:52,282 --> 01:05:55,284
what he 'd do was just stop whatever he was doing in the scene and go limp .

1346
01:05:55,368 --> 01:05:57,411
And that was his way of signaling his safety men

1347
01:05:57,495 --> 01:06:00,372
to swim over to him and give him an air hose .

1348
01:06:00,457 --> 01:06:02,833
Ricou tells a funny story about being out in the middle of the spring

1349
01:06:02,917 --> 01:06:04,918
and realizing he needed to go to the bathroom .

1350
01:06:05,003 --> 01:06:06,837
Somebody offered to take him ashore in a boat ,

1351
01:06:06,921 --> 01:06:09,381
but Ricou said , " No , don 't bother , " he 'd just swim ashore .

1352
01:06:09,466 --> 01:06:12,134
He swam underwater , because he said swimming underwater

1353
01:06:12,218 --> 01:06:14,094
was sometimes easier than swimming on the surface ,

1354
01:06:14,179 --> 01:06:16,972
and he got to a ladder that was on the dock next to shore .

1355
01:06:17,056 --> 01:06:20,184
He climbs up the ladder , still dressed as The Gill Man , needless to say ,

1356
01:06:20,268 --> 01:06:21,935
and all of a sudden , right there in front of him ,

1357
01:06:22,020 --> 01:06:23,687
was some lady and her little girl .

1358
01:06:23,771 --> 01:06:25,481
And the little girl started screaming .

1359
01:06:25,565 --> 01:06:27,816
Rico said , " She started screaming and running .

1360
01:06:27,901 --> 01:06:30,360
" And the mother went after her , and I went after both of them ,

1361
01:06:30,445 --> 01:06:35,032
" trying to say , ' Hey , hey , it 's okay . It 's okay . ' But me saying ' it 's okay ' didn 't do a thing .

1362
01:06:35,116 --> 01:06:37,242
" They took off and that 's the last I saw of them .

1363
01:06:37,327 --> 01:06:40,037
" But I never went out of my way to try and scare anybody , no .

1364
01:06:40,121 --> 01:06:43,957
" From then on , I made sure I kept the head off whenever I came ashore . "

1365
01:06:44,042 --> 01:06:47,503
Ben Chapman , on the other hand , liked to occasionally scare people .

1366
01:06:47,587 --> 01:06:50,672
He says that a lot of folks used to come and visit the sets and watch them shoot ,

1367
01:06:50,757 --> 01:06:53,300
including guests of people who worked at Universal .

1368
01:06:53,384 --> 01:06:55,844
And what Ben would do when he knew people were coming was

1369
01:06:55,929 --> 01:06:57,471
he 'd swim out to the middle of Park Lake

1370
01:06:57,555 --> 01:07:01,099
and just float there , with just the top of a helmet and the eyes showing .

1371
01:07:01,184 --> 01:07:03,769
He 'd stay like that and watch until he saw the people arrive ,

1372
01:07:03,853 --> 01:07:05,145
and then he 'd swim under the water

1373
01:07:05,230 --> 01:07:07,439
and come bursting up through the water like a porpoise ,

1374
01:07:07,524 --> 01:07:10,651
and then go under again . He 'd make it hard for them to really see him .

1375
01:07:10,777 --> 01:07:12,778
" Then I would swim in towards them

1376
01:07:12,862 --> 01:07:14,988
" with just the eyes and the top of the head showing .

1377
01:07:15,073 --> 01:07:17,908
" I 'd swim in this way , getting as close to the shore as I could .

1378
01:07:17,992 --> 01:07:20,369
" Of course , it 's becoming shallower and shallower .

1379
01:07:20,453 --> 01:07:22,704
" I 'd swim in to where it was about a foot-and-a-half deep ,

1380
01:07:22,789 --> 01:07:24,665
" and then all of a sudden , I would stand right up and ... "

1381
01:07:24,749 --> 01:07:26,208
Ben let out a roar .

1382
01:07:26,292 --> 01:07:28,961
Ben said some of the guests would just about wet their pants .

1383
01:07:32,632 --> 01:07:34,424
Audiences loved the underwater scenes .

1384
01:07:34,509 --> 01:07:37,010
Bob Burns saw Creature in 3D when it was new ,

1385
01:07:37,095 --> 01:07:40,013
and he says that in the underwater scenes , the water seemed to disappear ,

1386
01:07:40,098 --> 01:07:42,140
and it looked like The Gill Man was just floating in midair

1387
01:07:42,267 --> 01:07:45,143
in front of the screen in the theater . He said it was just amazing .

1388
01:07:45,562 --> 01:07:46,728
There was a preview of Creature

1389
01:07:46,813 --> 01:07:50,649
at the United Artists Theatre in Los Angeles on January 7 , 1954 ,

1390
01:07:50,817 --> 01:07:54,403
and on the cards the people were asked to fill out , one of the questions was ,

1391
01:07:54,487 --> 01:07:56,196
" Which scene did you like most ? "

1392
01:07:56,281 --> 01:07:58,740
Well , the underwater scenes were the hands-down winner .

1393
01:07:58,825 --> 01:08:01,243
Funnily enough , talk about a sign of changing times ,

1394
01:08:01,327 --> 01:08:04,454
in answer to the question , " Which scenes , if any , did you dislike ? "

1395
01:08:04,539 --> 01:08:06,999
More than one person complained about Kay 's line ,

1396
01:08:07,083 --> 01:08:09,418
" David and I are together all the time anyway . "

1397
01:08:09,502 --> 01:08:12,045
To them , that meant that they were living together without being married ,

1398
01:08:12,130 --> 01:08:13,755
and they squawked about that .

1399
01:08:13,840 --> 01:08:16,466
One person said he disliked all of it , especially

1400
01:08:16,551 --> 01:08:20,137
" when Kay made like Esther Williams , not the time or place for it . "

1401
01:08:20,471 --> 01:08:21,722
Some of the other comments :

1402
01:08:21,806 --> 01:08:24,099
" Most exciting picture I have ever witnessed . "

1403
01:08:24,183 --> 01:08:26,643
" Give us more pictures like this , only in color . "

1404
01:08:26,728 --> 01:08:29,521
" I 'm a bundle of nerves . I 'll never go swimming in a lagoon . "

1405
01:08:29,606 --> 01:08:32,357
" The spookiest picture I have ever seen , and screamed throughout . "

1406
01:08:33,192 --> 01:08:34,776
One person complained the plot

1407
01:08:34,861 --> 01:08:36,278
was a little too much like The Thing ,

1408
01:08:36,362 --> 01:08:38,113
and believe it or not , there is some truth in that .

1409
01:08:38,239 --> 01:08:40,824
But nobody mentioned King Kong , which was the inspiration .

1410
01:08:40,908 --> 01:08:42,117
As I mentioned before ,

1411
01:08:42,201 --> 01:08:45,579
watch Creature and Revenge back-to-back next time , and you can 't miss it .

1412
01:08:45,705 --> 01:08:48,248
As a matter of fact , one early script of Revenge of the Creature

1413
01:08:48,333 --> 01:08:51,627
ended with The Gill Man being shot , not by the sheriff 's posse ,

1414
01:08:51,711 --> 01:08:55,130
but by a guy , or guys , shooting at him from a helicopter ,

1415
01:08:55,214 --> 01:08:57,591
just like the biplane pilots who killed King Kong .

1416
01:09:00,011 --> 01:09:02,512
Maybe we won 't have to fight him on his own terms after all .

1417
01:09:02,597 --> 01:09:05,140
WEAVER : Speaking of the preview , or one of the previews anyway ,

1418
01:09:05,224 --> 01:09:09,186
Arthur Ross , who co-wrote the picture , told me an interesting anecdote about it .

1419
01:09:09,270 --> 01:09:12,064
A fellow named Ed Muhl was the head of production at Universal ,

1420
01:09:12,148 --> 01:09:13,482
and according to Ross ,

1421
01:09:13,566 --> 01:09:16,318
" He sensed something in Creature that nobody else did

1422
01:09:16,402 --> 01:09:17,944
" and they sort of laughed at him ,

1423
01:09:18,029 --> 01:09:20,530
" but he said it was going to be an absolute smash .

1424
01:09:20,615 --> 01:09:23,659
" For the preview of Creature , there were three buses filled with people .

1425
01:09:23,743 --> 01:09:27,371
" They took us to dinner at the Beachcomber , and then downtown for the preview .

1426
01:09:27,455 --> 01:09:30,332
" Ed made every executive , every contract producer ,

1427
01:09:30,416 --> 01:09:33,752
" every contract director come watch Creature , because , he said ,

1428
01:09:33,836 --> 01:09:37,839
" ' This is going to be the pattern and form and intent of our future films . '

1429
01:09:37,924 --> 01:09:42,552
" At that point , Harry Essex and I looked at each other and said , ' He must be crazy ! " '

1430
01:09:42,637 --> 01:09:48,475
Well , Ed Muhl turned out not to be crazy . Creature cost Universal $ 463,700 ,

1431
01:09:48,559 --> 01:09:51,937
and by the end of 1954 , it had already grossed three million .

1432
01:09:53,481 --> 01:09:56,358
Another negative review of Creature came from the Los Angeles Daily News .

1433
01:09:56,442 --> 01:09:59,736
" As the action unreels with the speed of an anchor being hoisted ,

1434
01:09:59,821 --> 01:10:02,155
" Miss Adams changes bathing suits several times ,

1435
01:10:02,240 --> 01:10:04,574
" walks woodenly and persistently into danger ,

1436
01:10:04,659 --> 01:10:09,287
" and screams so often and so loud that little babes in the audience grew pale . "

1437
01:10:09,789 --> 01:10:13,959
Julie Adams , who shot this scene on her 27th birthday , has just screamed again .

1438
01:10:14,043 --> 01:10:15,377
If I was with Bissell , I 'd be more worried

1439
01:10:15,461 --> 01:10:17,295
about Richard Carlson swinging that bat around

1440
01:10:17,380 --> 01:10:18,964
than I 'd be about the creature .

1441
01:10:19,257 --> 01:10:21,508
When Bissell 's young daughters saw Creature in a theater ,

1442
01:10:21,592 --> 01:10:24,094
this scene came on , and one of ' em stood up in the middle of the theater

1443
01:10:24,178 --> 01:10:25,470
and screamed , " It 's going after Daddy ! "

1444
01:10:25,555 --> 01:10:27,514
He 'll be back .

1445
01:10:27,598 --> 01:10:29,099
WEAVER : I mentioned before that Milicent Patrick

1446
01:10:29,183 --> 01:10:30,559
was one of the designers of The Gill Man

1447
01:10:30,643 --> 01:10:34,396
and actually worked on the suit itself , and then , because she was a looker ,

1448
01:10:34,480 --> 01:10:37,649
Universal sent her out on a tour to drum up interest in the movie .

1449
01:10:37,734 --> 01:10:41,236
Universal was going to promote Patrick as " the beauty who created the beast , "

1450
01:10:41,320 --> 01:10:44,656
but Bud Westmore , protecting his turf , took exception to that .

1451
01:10:44,741 --> 01:10:47,993
Westmore claimed that the creation of the creature was completely his own work .

1452
01:10:48,161 --> 01:10:50,162
Westmore , lying through his teeth ,

1453
01:10:50,246 --> 01:10:53,540
said that many people had been involved in the original sketches and thinking ,

1454
01:10:53,624 --> 01:10:56,918
but had all dropped out of the project when it became difficult and complex .

1455
01:10:57,003 --> 01:11:00,505
As a result , he said , he spent four or five weeks himself

1456
01:11:00,590 --> 01:11:03,133
developing the model which was eventually used .

1457
01:11:03,217 --> 01:11:07,012
So , at a staff meeting , they talked about this and they decided to call Milicent Patrick

1458
01:11:07,096 --> 01:11:09,681
" the beauty who lives with the beasts , " instead .

1459
01:11:09,766 --> 01:11:11,683
But even that didn 't satisfy Westmore .

1460
01:11:11,768 --> 01:11:15,687
A couple days later , he barged into the office of an executive named Sam Israel

1461
01:11:15,772 --> 01:11:17,898
and said that he resented the whole project .

1462
01:11:17,982 --> 01:11:21,485
Sam Israel wrote in a memo , " He is perturbed by the fact that Milicent

1463
01:11:21,569 --> 01:11:25,322
" will claim credit for what he has achieved in putting these monsters together ,

1464
01:11:25,406 --> 01:11:28,074
" and he would like us to make sure that , as far as possible ,

1465
01:11:28,159 --> 01:11:31,203
" the interviews stress the fact that he is the one who supervises

1466
01:11:31,287 --> 01:11:33,747
" the creation of these creatures from start to finish .

1467
01:11:33,831 --> 01:11:38,460
" Milicent 's job , he says , is merely to put his ideas in the form of sketches .

1468
01:11:38,544 --> 01:11:40,086
" Frankly , I am getting a little fed up

1469
01:11:40,171 --> 01:11:42,631
" with these squawks from people who are paid to do a job .

1470
01:11:42,715 --> 01:11:46,259
" And from what I hear , Milicent 's contribution in this work is very important . "

1471
01:11:47,762 --> 01:11:50,013
Well , Milicent Patrick went on a whirlwind tour .

1472
01:11:50,181 --> 01:11:53,475
She did a jillion print interviews , and she appeared on radio and TV .

1473
01:11:53,559 --> 01:11:55,310
She was on the Today Show with Dave Garroway

1474
01:11:55,394 --> 01:11:58,480
and she made personal appearances at theaters that were showing Creature ,

1475
01:11:58,564 --> 01:12:00,732
and busybody Bud Westmore made it his business

1476
01:12:00,817 --> 01:12:03,652
to get hold of newspapers from a number of the cities she had visited .

1477
01:12:03,736 --> 01:12:07,072
He discovered that she was being credited as the designer of the creature ,

1478
01:12:07,156 --> 01:12:10,951
and he made it known that he wasn 't going to use her as a sketch artist anymore .

1479
01:12:11,035 --> 01:12:13,495
Some of the suits at Universal tried to make Westmore understand

1480
01:12:13,579 --> 01:12:16,164
that she herself did everything possible to credit him ,

1481
01:12:16,249 --> 01:12:17,833
and if there was any blame in the matter ,

1482
01:12:17,917 --> 01:12:20,585
it was Universal 's fault or the newspapers ' fault .

1483
01:12:20,670 --> 01:12:23,839
One of the executives , who was exchanging memos with another executive

1484
01:12:23,923 --> 01:12:25,298
about this situation , wrote ,

1485
01:12:25,383 --> 01:12:27,843
" It would be most unfair if Westmore were to discontinue

1486
01:12:27,927 --> 01:12:31,137
" using the services of Patrick as a result of his pique .

1487
01:12:31,222 --> 01:12:34,057
" The facts are that we haven 't had a more cooperative person on tour

1488
01:12:34,141 --> 01:12:36,184
" than Miss Patrick . And in every interview ,

1489
01:12:36,269 --> 01:12:38,603
" she made it clear that she worked for Bud Westmore .

1490
01:12:38,688 --> 01:12:40,564
" And she plugged Westmore as head of makeup ,

1491
01:12:40,648 --> 01:12:43,149
" and followed the instructions that we gave to her .

1492
01:12:43,234 --> 01:12:47,779
" Patrick conducted herself beyond any criticism from any point of view . "

1493
01:12:47,864 --> 01:12:51,074
I 've got a copy of Millicent Patrick 's résumé , and unfortunately ,

1494
01:12:51,158 --> 01:12:54,077
it doesn 't look like she ever worked as a sketch artist at Universal again .

1495
01:13:12,763 --> 01:13:14,347
This is supposed to be a very dramatic moment .

1496
01:13:14,432 --> 01:13:16,933
Julie Adams is so glad Richard Carlson is safe ,

1497
01:13:17,018 --> 01:13:18,977
but it 's so obvious she doesn 't wanna get wet ,

1498
01:13:19,061 --> 01:13:21,563
I 'm sure because that lake water was probably cold .

1499
01:13:21,647 --> 01:13:23,231
And it was definitely cold at night .

1500
01:13:23,316 --> 01:13:26,359
Richard Denning remembers shooting out in the lake one cold October night .

1501
01:13:26,444 --> 01:13:28,695
They were out in the water and they were tired , and it was late ,

1502
01:13:28,779 --> 01:13:32,574
and a prop man kept bringing them brandy to keep them from freezing to death .

1503
01:13:32,658 --> 01:13:35,076
" It worked great , and we were going just fine ,

1504
01:13:35,161 --> 01:13:37,662
" and then we finally wrap it up and we go to the dressing rooms .

1505
01:13:37,747 --> 01:13:39,789
" They 're nice and warm , and all of a sudden ... "

1506
01:13:39,874 --> 01:13:41,583
And Denning starts slurring his words here .

1507
01:13:41,667 --> 01:13:43,835
" ... that brandy just hit us like a sledgehammer .

1508
01:13:43,961 --> 01:13:47,422
" But out in the cold and the wet and wind , it didn 't bother us at all . "

1509
01:13:47,840 --> 01:13:50,425
Ricou Browning had the same thing happen thousands of miles away .

1510
01:13:50,509 --> 01:13:53,428
He says everybody was trying to be nice , and they kept bringing him brandy ,

1511
01:13:53,512 --> 01:13:55,889
and pretty soon , they were working with a drunken creature .

1512
01:13:55,973 --> 01:13:58,099
So Ricou figured he 'd better cut that out .

1513
01:13:58,225 --> 01:13:59,809
DAVID : She 's coming . Back .

1514
01:14:00,978 --> 01:14:03,313
WEAVER : I think the idea of a drunken creature is kind of cute .

1515
01:14:03,689 --> 01:14:06,942
Julie Adams has kind of a soft spot for the creature in her heart , I guess .

1516
01:14:07,026 --> 01:14:11,154
She told me , " The creature scared people , but there was also a sweetness about it .

1517
01:14:11,238 --> 01:14:12,530
" In the real classics ,

1518
01:14:12,615 --> 01:14:15,325
" there always is that feeling of compassion for the monster .

1519
01:14:15,409 --> 01:14:17,827
" I think maybe it touches something in ourselves ,

1520
01:14:17,912 --> 01:14:19,663
" maybe the darker parts of ourselves

1521
01:14:19,747 --> 01:14:22,958
" that long to be loved and think they really can 't ever be loved .

1522
01:14:23,042 --> 01:14:24,626
" It strikes a chord within us .

1523
01:14:24,710 --> 01:14:26,378
" That 's what Creature from the Black Lagoon did . "

1524
01:14:26,462 --> 01:14:27,879
DAVID : Stand by to pull her up with the winch .

1525
01:14:28,881 --> 01:14:30,131
Pull her in .

1526
01:14:30,216 --> 01:14:31,841
( WINCH WHIRRING )

1527
01:14:34,845 --> 01:14:36,721
DAVID : That 's it .

1528
01:14:37,306 --> 01:14:38,431
Whoa !

1529
01:14:38,724 --> 01:14:41,101
I 'll go down and unfasten it .

1530
01:14:42,728 --> 01:14:44,562
- Give me slack ! - ( GROWLING )

1531
01:14:44,814 --> 01:14:45,814
WEAVER : We 're about to get a good look at

1532
01:14:45,898 --> 01:14:48,191
the creature 's gills pulsating as he breathes .

1533
01:14:48,275 --> 01:14:51,861
How they did that was , rubber tubes came into the back of Ben Chapman 's helmet ,

1534
01:14:51,946 --> 01:14:54,155
through the dorsal fin , and ran into the gills ,

1535
01:14:54,240 --> 01:14:56,366
where there were little balloons or air pockets .

1536
01:14:56,450 --> 01:14:57,659
On the other end of the hose ,

1537
01:14:57,743 --> 01:15:00,286
there was a guy sitting off camera with a little hand pump .

1538
01:15:00,413 --> 01:15:04,249
Chapman and this other fellow coordinated and worked together to create the effect .

1539
01:15:04,333 --> 01:15:05,875
Chapman would open his mouth and gasp

1540
01:15:05,960 --> 01:15:09,045
at the same time that the hand-pump guy would pump the gills .

1541
01:15:09,130 --> 01:15:12,257
That way , it looked like the gasping was what made the gills flare out .

1542
01:15:13,509 --> 01:15:16,428
Julie Adams is replaced in this shot by stuntwoman Polly Burson ,

1543
01:15:16,512 --> 01:15:18,179
who got swept off the boat by Ben Chapman .

1544
01:15:18,723 --> 01:15:21,558
No , don 't shoot , don 't shoot ! You might hit Kay .

1545
01:15:21,767 --> 01:15:23,518
WEAVER : And now we cut to a scene shot in Florida ,

1546
01:15:23,602 --> 01:15:26,771
Ricou Browning swimming underwater with Ginger Stanley in his arms .

1547
01:15:28,107 --> 01:15:29,941
That was our next-to-last shot at Ricou .

1548
01:15:30,359 --> 01:15:33,653
From now on , it 'll be Ben Chapman right up until the last shot of the movie .

1549
01:15:33,738 --> 01:15:36,239
Ricou Browning was recently profiled in People magazine

1550
01:15:36,323 --> 01:15:37,574
in connection with Creature .

1551
01:15:37,658 --> 01:15:40,368
But he says he 's more proud of a lot of the other things he 's done .

1552
01:15:40,453 --> 01:15:43,621
He writes , he 's directed , he created the TV series Flipper ,

1553
01:15:43,706 --> 01:15:47,042
he directed the underwater parts of Thunderball , and to him ,

1554
01:15:47,126 --> 01:15:49,169
Creature was just another movie , just another job .

1555
01:15:49,712 --> 01:15:53,006
Ben Chapman , on the other hand , revels in having played The Gill Man ,

1556
01:15:53,090 --> 01:15:55,967
and he regularly appears at sci-fi cons and autograph shows .

1557
01:15:56,427 --> 01:15:59,179
He 's also given a lot of thought to what made The Gill Man so popular .

1558
01:15:59,638 --> 01:16:03,224
Here 's a little bit of music from The Wolf Man as Richard Carlson climbs out of the water .

1559
01:16:03,893 --> 01:16:07,228
Chapman says that if you look back at all the thrillers that Universal made ,

1560
01:16:07,313 --> 01:16:11,357
The Phantom , The Hunchback , Frankenstein , Dracula , The Mummy , and Creature ,

1561
01:16:11,442 --> 01:16:14,110
" they were all very successful . And why ?

1562
01:16:14,195 --> 01:16:16,988
" If you 'll think about it , they were all Beauty and the Beast .

1563
01:16:17,073 --> 01:16:20,784
" The phantom was in love with the girl , the hunchback loved his Esmeralda ,

1564
01:16:20,868 --> 01:16:23,495
" the Mummy had his princess . They would never hurt the girl ,

